const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "F", "2": "E", "3": "A", "4": "A", "5": "B", "6": "F", "7": "C", "8": "B", "9": "E", "10": "D", "11": "D", "12": "D", "13": "D", "14": "D", "15": "E", "16": "E", "17": "C", "18": "C", "19": "C", "20": "E", "21": "A", "22": "D", "23": "E", "24": "C", "25": "B", "26": "F", "27": "B", "28": "D", "29": "B", "30": "B", "31": "E", "32": "B", "33": "B", "34": "E", "35": "B", "36": "G", "37": "F", "38": "E", "39": "D", "40": "B", "41": "F", "42": "A", "43": "B", "44": "A", "45": "B", "46": "A", "47": "E", "48": "B", "49": "C", "50": "C"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is right lateral medullary infarction. Lateral medullary syndrome, or Wallenberg syndrome, is characterized by a distinct combination of symptoms, including vertigo, ipsilateral facial sensory loss, contralateral body sensory loss, dysphagia, and Horner syndrome.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Wallenberg syndrome is a neurologic disorder caused by infarction in the lateral medulla.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Wallenberg syndrome is characterized by a distinct pattern of neurologic deficits resulting from an infarction in the lateral medulla oblongata, most often from occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery or vertebral artery. Patients typically exhibit ipsilateral facial sensory loss of pain and temperature, resulting from damage to the spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus. Contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the body occurs as a result of the involvement of the lateral spinothalamic tract. Dysphagia, hoarseness, and a diminished gag reflex arise from involvement of the nucleus ambiguus, affecting the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves. Additionally, vestibular nuclei involvement causes vertigo and nystagmus, and disruption of the descending sympathetic fibers leads to ipsilateral Horner syndrome, presenting with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient has a history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and coronary artery disease. He reports not taking his daily aspirin for the last 3 weeks due to intermittent epigastric pain and nausea. He is found to have right-sided Horner syndrome, right-sided gaze nystagmus, right-sided ataxia, and slurred speech. These symptoms localize to a lesion in the right lateral medulla.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A and Choice B:</b> Acute left-sided labyrinthitis and acute right-sided labyrinthitis are incorrect. Labyrinthitis typically presents with vertigo and hearing loss, but it would not cause cerebellar or brainstem symptoms such as those associated with a stroke.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Basilar occlusion with cerebellar infarction is incorrect. While basilar artery occlusion can cause cerebellar infarction and present with vertigo, it often leads to severe symptoms like quadriplegia, coma, or locked-in syndrome, depending on the extent of the occlusion. Additionally, Horner syndrome would not be expected in this scenario.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Left lateral medullary infarction is incorrect. While a lateral medullary infarction could present with symptoms such as vertigo and nystagmus, it would typically cause ipsilateral symptoms to the side of the infarction. This patient has right-sided head and neck symptoms, making this answer incorrect.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most appropriate response by the physician to this patient with a serious illness diagnosis is to encourage him to involve his loved ones in his care and in conversations about the illness, as he will need their help and support during the recovery process.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Some patients request that information be withheld from themselves or their families.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> The physician should explore the motivation behind the patient's request. In cases when patients wish to withhold information from family about a serious illness, the physician should encourage the patient to share information about their condition with their family so that the family can provide support.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Though patients should be encouraged to share pertinent information with their family, patients with decision-making capacity around their condition can ultimately opt to prevent information sharing. It is unethical for the physician to lie to patients or family, but the physician may withhold information per the patient's request.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> \"I believe you do not have the capacity to make this decision at this time\" is inappropriate. This is a paternalistic statement that misses an opportunity to encourage the patient to involve his social supports. Additionally, despite his cognitive impairments, it is not clear whether the patient lacks decision-making capacity for this decision.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> \"I will tell your wife that you have sustained a stroke, in accordance with your wishes\" is premature and unethical. The physician should first encourage the patient to involve his social supports. Ethically, the physician should not lie to the patient's family about his condition.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> \"It is mandatory for me to consult with the hospital ethics committee before making this decision\" is inappropriate. The physician should first encourage this patient to involve his social supports. If the patient continues to opt against involving his family, lacks decision-making capacity, and there are no alternative caretaking options, the physician may consider involving the ethics committee to guide the physician's next steps.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> \"You should consider appointing a health care proxy to help you direct your care\" is not the most effective initial statement. While this statement is true, the physician should first encourage this patient to inform his social supports about the situation.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has herpes encephalitis (HE), which is caused by herpesvirus (also known as herpes simplex virus) infection and typically presents with acute fever, altered mental status, and focal neurologic symptoms. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis often shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, increased protein concentrations, and normal glucose concentrations.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with HE often present with acute onset of fever, headache, confusion, seizures, and focal neurologic deficits, frequently affecting the temporal lobes.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> HE is a severe viral infection of the central nervous system caused predominantly by herpesvirus 1 and occasionally by herpesvirus 2. CSF analysis in HE typically shows an increased leukocyte count (usually in the range of 10 to 500/mm³) with a lymphocytic predominance, as well as increased protein concentrations and normal glucose concentrations. MRI of the brain often shows abnormalities in the temporal lobes, which can include edema, hemorrhage, and necrosis.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Currently, there is no vaccine for HE, but decreasing the risk for herpesvirus infection through safe practices to avoid direct skin-to-skin transmission and awareness can help prevent its occurrence.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> The primary treatment for HE is intravenous acyclovir, initiated as soon as the condition is suspected.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with a 1-day history of increased fussiness and drowsiness. Birth history includes inconsistent maternal health care and vaccinations. CSF analysis shows normal glucose concentrations, increased protein concentrations, and no organisms on Gram stain, which is consistent with a viral infection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Escherichia coli is incorrect. CSF analysis in E coli meningitis typically shows increased leukocyte count with a predominance of neutrophils, increased protein concentrations, and decreased glucose concentrations.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Neisseria gonorrhoeae is incorrect. CSF findings in N gonorrhoeae infection usually include increased leukocyte count with neutrophilic predominance, increased protein concentrations, and decreased glucose concentrations, although CNS involvement is rare.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Neisseria meningitidis is incorrect. In N meningitidis meningitis, CSF analysis often shows a high leukocyte count with neutrophilic predominance, increased protein concentrations, and significantly decreased glucose concentrations.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Streptococcus agalactiae (group B) is incorrect. S agalactiae meningitis typically presents in infants, and CSF will show increased leukocyte count, increased protein concentrations, and decreased glucose concentrations.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has a concussion. A concussion is a mild traumatic brain injury characterized by a Glasgow Coma Scale score range of 13 to 15 with symptoms often including confusion, amnesia, and sometimes loss of consciousness, with young children potentially exhibiting irritability or balance disturbances.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> A concussion is a mild traumatic brain injury that occurs when an impact to the head or body causes the brain to move rapidly within the cranium.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Concussion, a mild traumatic brain injury, is diagnosed based on clinical criteria that include a history of head trauma followed by transient neurologic symptoms such as confusion, amnesia, or loss of consciousness. Physical examination may show signs such as irritability, balance disturbances, or changes in behavior, which are particularly relevant in pediatric patients who may not be able to articulate their symptoms. The Glasgow Coma Scale score for a concussion typically ranges from 13 to 15, indicating mild injury, though in very young children, the assessment may rely more on observational criteria because of communication limitations. Diagnostic imaging, such as a CT scan, is generally reserved for cases where there is a suspicion of more serious injury, such as skull fracture or intracranial hemorrhage, especially if symptoms persist or worsen. Management primarily involves cognitive and physical rest, with gradual return to normal activities guided by symptom resolution, and it is crucial to monitor for any signs of deterioration, as young children may have difficulty expressing their symptoms. Follow-up care should include education for caregivers on recognizing potential complications, such as postconcussion syndrome, and ensuring a safe environment to prevent further injury.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents shortly after falling and hitting her head, with positive loss of consciousness and transient lethargy. Examination shows an area of ecchymosis, no focal neurologic findings, and no abnormalities on imaging. These findings are consistent with a concussion.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, F, G, and H.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cephalohematoma is incorrect. Cephalohematoma is characterized by a firm, nonfluctuant swelling on a newborn's scalp, restricted by suture lines, typically manifesting hours to days postdelivery and generally resolving without intervention.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Cerebral contusion is incorrect. Cerebral contusions manifest as focal brain bruising caused by trauma, presenting with neurologic deficits and altered consciousness, and appearing on CT scan as areas of mixed density. CT scan performed on this patient shows no abnormalities.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Epidural hematoma is incorrect. Epidural hematoma involves blood accumulation between the dura mater and skull, often from middle meningeal artery rupture, typically exhibiting a \"lucid interval\" followed by rapid neurologic deterioration, seen as a biconvex hyperdensity on CT scan. This patient's CT scan shows no abnormalities.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Frontal skull fracture is incorrect. A frontal skull fracture is a reasonable concern in this patient given the mechanism of injury and bruising; however, this would have been seen on CT scan. Concussion is more likely.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Shaken baby syndrome is incorrect. Shaken baby syndrome, now referred to generally as abusive or inflicted head trauma, presents with subdural hematomas, retinal hemorrhages, and encephalopathy. This patient does not have these findings.</p><p><b>Choice G:</b> Skull fracture over the middle meningeal artery is incorrect. Fracture in this area can lead to epidural hematoma due to arterial damage; however, imaging would have identified this abnormality.</p><p><b>Choice H:</b> Subdural hematoma is incorrect. Subdural hematoma results from venous bleeding between the dura and arachnoid. It presents with headache, confusion, and hemiparesis, and is identified on CT scan as crescent-shaped hyperdensities.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Infantile epileptic spasms syndrome (IESS), also known as infantile spasms, often presents, as in this patient, with spasms in infancy with associated hypsarrhythmia identified on EEG, and with arrested psychomotor development. The syndrome does not respond to typical antiepileptic medications, so the most appropriate pharmacotherapy is adrenocorticotropic hormone.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> IESS presents with spasms that often develop in infants younger than 1 year. These spasms can involve the extremities as well as muscles of the neck and trunk.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> The pathogenesis of IESS is not well understood but may result from structural brain abnormalities, infection, or brain injury, or be secondary to genetic variants. In the initial stage of the condition, spasms may be infrequent and mild and may become more frequent with up to hundreds of spasms occurring over the course of 1 day. Spasms may decrease in the third stage of the disease and may be followed by the development of different types of seizures.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the underlying causes are unknown or nonspecific, there is no known way to prevent infantile spasms.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> IESS does not respond to typical antiepileptic medications. The syndrome is treated with hormonal therapy including adrenocorticotropic hormone.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The patient in this case presents with new onset, frequent spasms and EEG findings of hypsarrhythmia, which is characteristic of IESS. Initial treatment includes administration of adrenocorticotropic hormone.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Diazepam is incorrect. Diazepam is a benzodiazepine used in the treatment of anxiety, spasticity, and seizures. Adrenocorticotropic hormone is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for IESS.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Phenobarbital is not the most appropriate pharmacotherapy. Phenobarbital is a barbiturate and central nervous system depressant that has similar effects to alcohol and benzodiazepines. Phenobarbital is used for treatment of seizures; however, IESS does not respond to typical antiepileptic therapy.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Phenytoin is incorrect. Phenytoin is an anticonvulsant medication that acts as a voltage-gated sodium channel blocker. Phenytoin can be used in the emergent treatment of generalized tonic-clonic seizures, complex partial seizures, and status epilepticus.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is not the most appropriate pharmacotherapy. Vitamin B6 may be used to treat pyridoxine-dependent epilepsy, but adrenocorticotropic hormone is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy for IESS.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's presentation is consistent with cervical radiculopathy, and the most appropriate next step is ibuprofen therapy and rest of the neck and arm. Cervical radiculopathy is most commonly caused by intervertebral disc herniation and cervical spondylosis, with initial treatment often including NSAIDs to manage pain and inflammation associated with nerve root compression.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with cervical radiculopathy typically present with sharp or burning radicular pain, sensory abnormalities, and possible motor deficits following a dermatomal pattern.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Cervical radiculopathy results from nerve root dysfunction in the cervical spine, manifesting as pain, sensory abnormalities, and potentially motor deficits in the distribution of the affected nerve. Typically, this condition arises from mechanical compression or inflammation of the nerve roots, often due to intervertebral disc herniation, cervical spondylotic changes, or, less commonly, space-occupying lesions such as tumors. The pathophysiology involves both mechanical and inflammatory processes, where the mechanical deformation of the nerve root can lead to demyelination and axonal injury, while the release of proinflammatory cytokines exacerbates nerve irritation. Clinically, patients may present with radicular pain that is sharp or burning in nature, following a dermatomal pattern, often accompanied by paresthesia or hypoesthesia, and in some cases, myotomal weakness or atrophy, reflecting the specific roots involved, commonly C5 to C7. Diagnostic evaluation involves a thorough history and physical examination, with provocative maneuvers such as the Spurling test, and is supported by imaging modalities such as MRI, which provides detailed visualization of soft tissue structures to assess for disc pathology, foraminal compromise, and other structural anomalies. Electromyography and nerve conduction studies can further delineate the level of nerve involvement and exclude peripheral neuropathies, aiding in a comprehensive approach to diagnosis.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention of cervical radiculopathy can include maintaining good posture, engaging in regular neck-strengthening exercises, and avoiding repetitive neck strain to decrease the risk for cervical spine degeneration.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management of cervical radiculopathy may involve conservative approaches such as physical therapy, NSAIDs, and corticosteroid injections, with surgery considered for refractory cases or those with significant neurologic deficits.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with signs and symptoms consistent with radiculopathy. Given the duration of symptoms, age of the patient, and relative function level, it is appropriate to begin treatment with NSAIDs and rest.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Follow-up examination in 1 week is incorrect. This patient has signs and symptoms consistent with radiculopathy, and discomfort that is affecting her daily life. Pharmacotherapy should be initiated.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Cervical epidural administration of dexamethasone is incorrect. Epidural corticosteroid injection is not appropriate at this time, as it should be reserved for those for whom conservative treatment has failed or those who have more severe symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Electromyography and nerve conduction studies is incorrect. While this testing can provide information into a more specific level of diagnosis, the duration of this patient's symptoms makes it unlikely that nerve conduction studies or electromyography would capture any neurologic changes. Additionally, it is more appropriate to try conservative treatment with NSAIDs before moving to uncomfortable tests such as these.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Cervical laminectomy and fusion is incorrect. This patient has mild radiculopathy and has not tried conservative therapy. Surgical intervention should be reserved for those with bowel or bladder dysfunction, intractable pain, or weakness.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's presentation is consistent with hyperextension injury of the neck, or whiplash. Whiplash primarily affects the cervical spine, where rapid hyperextension and hyperflexion can lead to injury of the soft tissues, including muscles, ligaments, and intervertebral discs.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Whiplash, commonly resulting from motor vehicle collisions (MVCs), is a neck injury caused by a sudden acceleration-deceleration force.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Neck hyperextension injuries, in this case, whiplash, typically occur when a sudden force causes the neck to extend beyond its normal range of motion, often as a result of rear-end MVCs. This rapid extension and subsequent flexion of the cervical spine can lead to a variety of soft tissue injuries, including strain or sprain of the cervical muscles and ligaments, facet joint injuries, and, in some cases, intervertebral disc damage. Patients may present with a range of symptoms such as neck pain and stiffness, headaches, dizziness, and, occasionally, neurologic symptoms, such as paresthesia, if nerve roots are affected. Diagnosis primarily relies on clinical evaluation, though imaging such as x-rays or MRI may be used to assess more severe injuries or to rule out other conditions. Management typically involves a combination of pain control with NSAIDs or muscle relaxants, physical therapy to restore range of motion and strength, and patient education on posture and ergonomics to prevent further injury.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents after a low-speed MVC. He has neck soreness and mildly decreased range of motion with rotation but no other neurologic symptoms. This presentation is most consistent with whiplash.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Herniated disc is incorrect. A herniated disc often presents with significant pain that limits range of motion, as well as the possibility of radicular symptoms from nerve compression. This patient's symptoms are more consistent with whiplash.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Malingering is incorrect. Assessment of malingering requires a tactful approach and thorough examination. This patient has x-rays that show straightening of the normal cervical lordosis, and a recent history of MVC. This is congruent with a diagnosis of whiplash, not malingering.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Spondylolisthesis is incorrect. Spondylolisthesis is the anterior or posterior slipping of one vertebra on another. This patient's x-rays do not show this finding.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Vertebral artery dissection is incorrect. This patient presents after an MVC with moderate neck pain. A vertebral artery dissection would produce significant neurologic deficits.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Triptans, including sumatriptan, are often used for acute migraine relief, but are contraindicated in patients, like this one, with a history of myocardial infarction (MI), because of their potential to induce coronary vasoconstriction, which can exacerbate cardiac ischemia.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Migraines are a prevalent and debilitating neurologic condition characterized by severe, recurrent headaches, often accompanied by sensory disturbances. Triptans are a primary treatment option, but they require careful consideration of cardiovascular risk factors.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Migraines are a complex neurologic disorder characterized by intense, throbbing headaches often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and sensitivity to light and sound. These headaches typically last between 4 to 72 hours and can significantly impair daily functioning. The pathophysiology of migraines involves the activation of the trigeminovascular system and the release of inflammatory neuropeptides, which contribute to the dilation of intracranial blood vessels. Triptans, a class of medications commonly used to treat acute migraine attacks, work by selectively agonizing serotonin 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors, leading to vasoconstriction of cranial blood vessels and inhibition of proinflammatory neuropeptide release. However, triptans are contraindicated in patients with a history of MI because they can cause coronary vasoconstriction, potentially exacerbating ischemic heart conditions and increasing the risk for additional cardiac events.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with classic signs of migraines, including nausea, vomiting, light and noise sensitivity, as well as a defined potential trigger. Given her cardiac history, vasoconstrictive medications such as sumatriptan should be avoided to limit any further ischemic damage to the myocardium.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Amitriptyline is incorrect. Amitriptyline should be avoided in patients with recent MI because of its potential to cause acute cardiac arrhythmias and its anticholinergic effect. However, this patient's MI was 3 years ago, making amitriptyline considerable for use.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Aspirin is incorrect. Aspirin can be used for headache symptoms but is generally ineffective in patients with migraines. Aspirin should be avoided in patients with a history of bleeding disorders, not a history of MI.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Gabapentin is incorrect. Gabapentin is contraindicated in patients with severe renal impairment because it is primarily excreted by the kidneys. It is not contraindicated in patients with a history of MI and is generally not used in migraine management.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Verapamil is incorrect. Verapamil is contraindicated in patients with severe left ventricular dysfunction or heart block due to its negative inotropic and chronotropic effects. This patient does not display any signs or diagnostics indicative of left ventricular dysfunction.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Neuropsychiatric symptoms, such as paranoia, are relatively common in dementia, as seen in this patient. Management may include atypical antipsychotics, such as quetiapine therapy, to address aggressive behavior.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Vascular dementia refers to cognitive impairment that results from acute cerebral infarction secondary to underlying cerebrovascular or thromboembolic disease. Patients with vascular dementia typically possess a history of clinical strokes with consequent focal neurologic deficits and a stepwise decline in function. Some patients with neurocognitive disorders develop neuropsychiatric symptoms, such as paranoia and aggressive behavior.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> This patient possesses risk factors for cerebrovascular disease, such as hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and atherosclerotic disease (as evidenced by a carotid bruit, peripheral artery disease signs, and the need for coronary artery bypass grafting). Diagnosis of vascular dementia requires evidence of cerebrovascular disease (eg, clinical history of stroke or neuroimaging evidence of infarct) and cognitive impairment.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Primary prevention of stroke and vascular dementia includes a healthy diet, exercise, and avoidance of smoking.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management requires secondary prevention of stroke via blood pressure control, and in many cases, antiplatelet therapy. Management of aggressive behavior may involve atypical antipsychotic medications (eg, quetiapine) to calm patients.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Cholinesterase inhibitors and certain antidepressants can help decrease aggressive tendencies in the long term. However, antidepressants with anticholinergic activity (eg, amitriptyline) can worsen cognition and typically have an onset of action of weeks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Amitriptyline therapy is incorrect. Amitriptyline is a tricyclic antidepressant. Antidepressants can assist with aggressive behavior in the long term but are less effective at calming patients quickly. Also, antidepressants with anticholinergic activity can worsen cognition, making amitriptyline not appropriate for management of this patient's agitation and aggressive behavior.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Lorazepam is incorrect. Though benzodiazepines can assist with aggressive behavior in the moment, they are likely to worsen cognitive impairment.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Oxygen therapy at home is incorrect. Though there is emerging evidence for oxygen therapy in delaying the progression of dementia, Alzheimer type, it is not typically useful in addressing aggressive behavior associated with dementia.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) therapy is incorrect. Though vitamin B12 may assist in prevention of dementia, Alzheimer type, it is not typically useful in addressing aggressive behavior associated with dementia.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's symptoms are most likely caused by dysfunction in the mitochondria. Mitochondrial myopathy, encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes (MELAS) syndrome is an inherited mitochondrial disorder characterized by episodes of encephalopathy, headache, focal neurologic deficits, seizures, and myopathy with lactic acidosis. Imaging may show multiple infarcts in various stages of ischemic evolution.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> MELAS syndrome is an inherited mitochondrial disorder. Manifestations usually occur in childhood or adolescence after a period of normal early development. Features include episodic encephalopathy, headache, focal neurologic deficits (eg, stroke-like episodes), seizures, and myopathy with lactic acidosis.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Like all mitochondrial disorders, MELAS is maternally inherited, and heteroplasmy (ie, presence of different mitochondrial genomes within cells and individuals) confers significant variation in severity and age of onset. Because muscle and neurologic tissues are highly metabolically active, effects on these systems form the hallmarks of the disease. Episodes typically display a relapsing-remitting pattern, and patients experience progressive neurologic deterioration leading to functional impairment and dementia.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This early-adolescent patient with recurrent episodes of neurologic dysfunction and evidence of inadequate energy production in neural tissue (increased lactate in cerebrospinal fluid) most likely has MELAS syndrome. MRI findings typically show scattered infarcts in different stages of ischemic evolution, including the cortex, basal ganglia, and thalamus. Muscle biopsy will show ragged red fibers, caused by proliferation of the diseased mitochondria in an attempt to compensate for their inadequate function.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Endoplasmic reticulum is incorrect. The endoplasmic reticulum is an organelle involved in the synthesis, modification, and transport of proteins, as well as the synthesis of lipids and storage of calcium. Defects have been implicated in neurodegenerative disorders such as Alzheimer and Parkinson disease.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Erythrocytes is incorrect. There are many heritable disorders affecting the erythrocytes including sickle cell disease, thalassemias, and hereditary spherocytosis. Features vary, but stem from defective erythrocytes and can include anemia, hemolysis, splenomegaly, and pain crises.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Fibroblasts is incorrect. Fibroblasts are affected in Marfan syndrome, an autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder resulting in an inherited mutation of the FBN1 gene. Features include tall and thin stature, ocular lens dislocation, mitral valve prolapse, and aneurysms.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Lysosome is incorrect. Lysosomes are responsible for the degradation of a variety of substrates, including those brought into the cell through endocytosis or materials of the cell itself (eg, autophagy). Deficiencies in lysosomal enzymes lead to lysosomal storage disorders, which present broadly with neurocognitive decline, skeletal abnormalities, and craniofacial abnormalities.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most likely diagnosis for this patient presenting with signs of rapidly progressive dementia, involuntary muscle jerks, coordination difficulties, slurred speech, and emotional lability is Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD). CJD occurs due to abnormal protein folding resulting in widespread neuronal loss and spongiform changes throughout the brain.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> CJD is a rare, degenerative, and invariably fatal brain disorder characterized by rapid progression and severe neurologic decline. It is characterized by rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, and ataxia. Additional symptoms may include visual disturbances, behavior changes such as depression, and speech difficulty, among others.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> At the molecular level, CJD is caused by the accumulation of prion protein scrapies (PrPSc), which are aberrant isoforms of the normal cellular prion protein (PrPC). The misfolding of PrPC into PrPSc results in a conformational change characterized by an increase in beta sheet content, making these proteins resistant to proteolytic degradation. This accumulation leads to the formation of amyloid plaques, which disrupt normal neuronal function and induce neurodegeneration through mechanisms that are not fully understood but may involve oxidative stress, apoptosis, and inflammation. The propagation of PrPSc is autocatalytic, as it induces the misfolding of additional PrPC molecules, perpetuating the pathogenic cycle. Clinically, this molecular pathology manifests as rapidly progressive dementia, emotional lability, myoclonus, ataxia, and a spectrum of other neurologic symptoms, reflecting widespread neuronal loss and spongiform changes within the brain.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention of CJD focuses on avoiding exposure to contaminated neural tissue and implementing strict safety protocols in medical and surgical settings. Additionally, controlling livestock-related transmissions, particularly from cattle with bovine spongiform encephalopathy, is essential.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Currently, there is no cure for CJD. Treatment focuses on alleviating symptoms and providing supportive care to maintain patient comfort. Additionally, this patient may have grown up in or traveled frequently to England, given his recent trip for his mother's funeral, so he may have been exposed to prion at some point during his youth as the United Kingdom previously had the highest prevalence of CJD worldwide.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Rapidly progressive dementia, emotional lability, myoclonus, and imaging showing diffuse cortical atrophy are highly suggestive of CJD.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Beta-Adrenergic blocking agent toxicity is incorrect. While beta-adrenergic blocker therapy can cause symptoms that can be misinterpreted for dementia, this patient has been on a stable dose for 10 years and it would not explain the CT scan of the head showing diffuse cortical atrophy.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Dementia, Alzheimer type is incorrect. Dementia, Alzheimer type, can present with symptoms like those seen in this patient, including confusion, memory loss, imbalance, and emotional lability. However, the rapid progression of symptoms over just a few months would not be expected.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Dementia secondary to HIV infection is incorrect. Dementia secondary to HIV infection could present with rapidly progressive dementia and personality changes. However, this patient has no history indicative of HIV infection, making this unlikely.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Lewy body dementia is incorrect. Lewy body dementia typically presents with fluctuating cognitive impairment, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonian symptoms. Rapid progression of symptoms would be uncommon.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Parkinson disease is incorrect. Parkinson disease typically presents with motor symptoms, such as bradykinesia, resting tremor, and rigidity. Rapidly progressive dementia and emotional lability would be uncommon.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has spinal cord compression, which typically presents with neurologic examination findings including motor weakness, sensory level changes, hyperreflexia, and present Babinski sign.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Spinal cord compression often presents with a range of neurologic examination findings depending on the level and severity of the compression.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Patients may exhibit motor weakness, which can be noted as decreased muscle strength in the affected limbs, often following a myotomal pattern. Sensory deficits are common with loss of sensation, such as numbness or tingling in a dermatomal distribution below the level of compression. Hyperreflexia is frequently observed due to upper motor neuron involvement, along with the presence of pathologic reflexes, like Babinski sign. In severe cases, such as seen in this patient, those with spinal cord compression may experience bowel or bladder dysfunction, indicating significant compromise of the spinal cord pathways.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with incontinence, lower extremity weakness, hyperreflexia, and present Babinski sign. These findings point to an upper motor neuron lesion as a result of spinal cord compression, which only a central nervous system lesion would cause.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cauda equina syndrome is incorrect. Cauda equina syndrome often presents with low back pain, saddle anesthesia, bowel and bladder dysfunction, and varying degrees of lower extremity motor and sensory loss. This patient presents with upper motor neuron lesion signs and symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Myopathy is incorrect. Myopathy is often characterized by symmetrical proximal muscle weakness, often affecting the shoulders and hips, with little or no sensory involvement.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Peripheral neuropathy is incorrect. This typically presents with distal sensory loss, numbness, tingling, and weakness, often in a stocking-glove distribution. This patient reports no sensory loss or weakness in his hands.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Plexopathy is incorrect. Plexopathy presents with acute or subacute onset of weakness, sensory changes, and pain in the distribution of a specific nerve plexus, such as the brachial or lumbosacral plexus, often following trauma, inflammation, or compression. This patient presents with upper motor neuron lesion signs and symptoms.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has sensory ataxia. Sensory ataxia results from damage to proprioceptive pathways, causing unsteady gait and balance issues, often due to conditions like vitamin B12 (cobalamin) deficiency, tabes dorsalis, or as part of a paraneoplastic syndrome.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Sensory ataxia is characterized by impaired coordination and balance as a result of defective proprioceptive input to the central nervous system.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Sensory ataxia is a type of ataxia that arises from the disruption of proprioceptive input to the central nervous system, leading to impaired coordination and balance. It is primarily caused by damage to the dorsal columns of the spinal cord or peripheral nerves, which are responsible for transmitting sensory information about limb position and movement. Clinically, patients with sensory ataxia often present with a broad-based, unsteady gait and may have a present Romberg sign, indicating a reliance on visual cues to maintain balance. The condition is often associated with sensory deficits such as loss of vibration and joint position sense, particularly in the lower extremities. Common causes include conditions such as tabes dorsalis, vitamin B12 deficiency, chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, and as part of paraneoplastic syndromes from malignancy, each of which can disrupt the proprioceptive pathways necessary for coordination.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with difficulty walking, proximal muscle weakness, a productive cough, weight loss, significant smoking history, and physical examination that shows profound loss of sensation to vibration and position. These findings are consistent with sensory ataxia.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, G, and H.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Apractic gait is incorrect. Apractic gait is characterized by difficulty initiating and coordinating walking movements despite having the physical ability to do so, often due to brain lesions affecting motor planning.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Cerebellar ataxia is incorrect. Cerebellar ataxia results in a broad-based, unsteady gait with difficulty in coordination and balance, commonly caused by damage to the cerebellum.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Festinating gait is incorrect. Festinating gait is marked by short, shuffling steps that progressively increase in speed, often seen in Parkinson disease.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Idiopathic senile gait is incorrect. Idiopathic senile gait refers to the slow, cautious walking pattern in older individuals, characterized by decreased stride length and increased double support time, without a specific neurologic cause.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Myopathic gait is incorrect. Myopathic gait is characterized by a waddling motion caused by weakness of the pelvic girdle muscles, often seen in muscular dystrophies.</p><p><b>Choice G:</b> Spastic gait is incorrect. Spastic gait is characterized by stiff, jerky movements with legs held together and toes dragging, often resulting from upper motor neuron lesions.</p><p><b>Choice H:</b> Trendelenburg gait is incorrect. Trendelenburg gait occurs when the pelvis drops on the side of the lifted leg during walking because of weakness of the hip abductor muscles on the stance leg side.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) is most likely to have prevented progression of this patient's disease. Vitamin B12 deficiency, often caused by pernicious anemia, can lead to macrocytic anemia and severe neurologic complications caused by impaired DNA synthesis and myelin production.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Severe vitamin B12 deficiency can lead to profound neurologic impairments, including peripheral neuropathy, cognitive disturbances, and in extreme cases, an unarousable state due to significant disruption of nerve function and myelin integrity.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Vitamin B12 deficiency is a common complication after partial gastrectomy because of decreased production of intrinsic factor, necessary for vitamin B12 absorption in the ileum. This deficiency can lead to pernicious anemia, characterized by fatigue, pallor, and macrocytic anemia caused by impaired red blood cell production. Neurologic symptoms can include peripheral neuropathy, cognitive disturbances, memory impairments, and, in severe cases, altered mental status or unresponsiveness. These symptoms occur because vitamin B12 is essential for maintaining the integrity of the myelin sheath and proper nerve function, and its deficiency can lead to significant neurologic impairment. In extreme cases, patients may become unarousable due to severe disruption of central nervous system function.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Vitamin B12 deficiency is prevented by ensuring adequate dietary intake of vitamin B12-rich foods such as meat and dairy, especially in populations at risk such as older adults, those with gastrectomy history, or those with restrictive diets.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of vitamin B12 deficiency involves repletion of vitamin B12. The route of repletion, whether oral or intramuscular, can be chosen dependent on the underlying cause of deficiency.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with increasing lethargy and an inability to be aroused. Notable history includes worsening memory, treatment for anemia with both iron and folic acid, and partial gastrectomy 14 years prior. All of these factors raise concern for vitamin B12 deficiency caused by pernicious anemia as the result of her gastrectomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Levodopa is incorrect. Levodopa would be administered to a patient with Parkinson disease, which often presents with symptoms such as tremors, bradykinesia, and muscle rigidity.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Penicillin is incorrect. Penicillin would be used in a patient with bacterial meningitis, which would present with symptoms such as fever, neck stiffness, and altered mental status.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Thyroid hormone is incorrect. Thyroid hormone replacement would be necessary for a patient with hypothyroidism, who might present with fatigue, weight gain, and cold intolerance. This patient's history of gastrectomy makes vitamin B12 deficiency more likely.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Vitamin B1 (thiamine) is incorrect. Vitamin B1 supplementation is crucial for patients at risk for Wernicke encephalopathy, which presents with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia, especially in those with alcohol use disorder.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH). IIH is characterized by increased intracranial pressure without an identifiable cause, predominantly affecting women of childbearing age who are overweight.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with IIH typically present with symptoms such as headaches, visual disturbances, and pulsatile tinnitus, accompanied by physical examination findings like papilledema.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> IIH, also known as pseudotumor cerebri, is characterized by increased intracranial pressure without an identifiable cause, often presenting in women who are overweight and of childbearing age. Clinically, patients typically report headaches, transient visual obscurations, and pulsatile tinnitus, while physical examination may show papilledema and visual field defects. The pathophysiology of IIH is not completely understood, but it is thought to involve impaired cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) absorption or increased CSF production. Diagnostic criteria for IIH require the presence of increased intracranial pressure measured via lumbar puncture, normal CSF composition, and no evidence of mass lesions or hydrocephalus on neuroimaging. Neuroimaging, particularly MRI and MR venography, is essential to exclude secondary causes of increased intracranial pressure, such as venous sinus thrombosis or structural brain abnormalities.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> While specific prevention strategies for IIH are not well-established, maintaining a healthy weight may help decrease the risk in susceptible individuals.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of IIH often includes weight management, pharmacological interventions such as acetazolamide, and in some cases, surgical procedures like optic nerve sheath fenestration or CSF shunting.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with evidence of increased intracranial pressure including headache, visual disturbances, and papilledema. Neuroimaging does not show hydrocephalus, making IIH the most likely cause.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cerebellar tumor is incorrect. Patients with a cerebellar tumor often present with ataxia, dysmetria, and nystagmus. A lumbar puncture may show increased intracranial pressure as a result of obstructive mechanisms but will typically show normal CSF composition.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Choroid plexus papilloma is incorrect. Choroid plexus papilloma may manifest with symptoms of increased intracranial pressure such as headaches and vomiting. However, lumbar puncture would show increased CSF protein and possibly increased CSF production, leading to increased opening pressure. Results of this patient's CSF analysis are within normal limits.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Obstructive hydrocephalus is incorrect. Obstructive hydrocephalus can present with symptoms very similar to IIH. However, on neuroimaging, ventriculomegaly due to the outflow obstruction of CSF would be visualized, and it is not in this case.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Viral meningitis is incorrect. Patients with viral meningitis typically present with headache, fever, and neck stiffness, and a lumbar puncture would show clear CSF with increased white blood cell count, normal glucose concentrations, and slightly increased protein. Results of this patient's CSF analysis and MRI are more consistent with IIH.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's postoperative intraparenchymal hemorrhagic stroke is most likely the result of hypertension, which is the leading modifiable risk factor for hemorrhagic stroke as it causes degenerative changes in cerebral vasculature that increase the risk for vessel rupture.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with hemorrhagic stroke often present with sudden onset of neurologic deficits, such as weakness, altered consciousness, or headache.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Hypertension is the most significant risk factor for hemorrhagic stroke due to its profound impact on the structural integrity of cerebral blood vessels. Chronic high blood pressure causes degenerative changes, such as lipohyalinosis and fibrinoid necrosis, which weaken the walls of small penetrating arteries. These pathological alterations increase the likelihood of vessel rupture, particularly in deep brain structures, leading to hemorrhagic stroke. Additionally, hypertension contributes to the formation of microaneurysms, such as Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms, which are prone to rupture under sustained pressure.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Effective management and control of blood pressure through lifestyle modifications and antihypertensive medications are crucial in preventing hemorrhagic strokes related to hypertension.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Acute treatment of hemorrhagic strokes focuses on stabilizing the patient, managing intracranial pressure, and addressing hypertension, often involving surgical intervention to evacuate hematomas and careful blood pressure control.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents 2 days after carotid endarterectomy with evidence of a left-sided intraparenchymal hemorrhagic stroke. The greatest risk factor for development of hemorrhagic strokes is hypertension.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Intracranial aneurysm is incorrect. Intracranial aneurysms typically cause subarachnoid hemorrhages on rupture, whereas intraparenchymal hemorrhages are more likely due to other vascular issues within the brain tissue itself.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intracranial tumor is incorrect. While intracranial tumors can occasionally cause intraparenchymal bleeding, this is an uncommon occurrence and this patient's imaging does not show any masses.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Plaque embolization is incorrect. Plaque embolization generally results in ischemic events by occluding cerebral arteries, not intraparenchymal hemorrhages.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Platelet dysfunction is incorrect. Platelet dysfunction can increase the severity of bleeding but is less likely to be the primary cause of spontaneous intraparenchymal hemorrhages.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Uncal herniation should be expected in this patient who presents with a progressive neurologic decompensation associated with a unilateral fixed and dilated pupil. Herniation likely occurred secondary to cerebral edema following his recent infarction.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Uncal herniation presents with pupillary dilation, ptosis, and oculomotor nerve palsy, in addition to obtundation.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Uncal herniation often presents after a neurologic insult, such as a hemorrhagic or ischemic stroke, or a traumatic brain injury. Ultimately, herniation results from increased intracranial pressure (ICP), either due to intracranial hemorrhage or cerebral edema, which causes the uncus to herniate under the tentorium cerebelli and compress the midbrain. This compression most frequently affects the oculomotor nerve.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention includes prompt diagnosis of brain injury and cerebral edema, recognition of changes in mental status, and limiting increases in ICP with Fowler position, hypertonic saline or mannitol administration, avoidance of hypercarbia, normothermia, and sedation. Measurements of ICP monitors or drainage of cerebrospinal fluid with extraventricular drains may also prove useful and allow for prompt recognition of impending herniation.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> In the case of herniation, mannitol and hypertonic saline, hyperventilation, sedation, and drainage of cerebrospinal fluid via an extraventricular drain may temporize the situation. Ultimately, herniation requires surgical interventions, such as decompressive burr hole surgery or craniotomy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Unilateral pupillary dilation is a moderately specific finding for uncal herniation, especially when temporally following a predisposing insult (eg, infarction). A new independent subarachnoid hemorrhage or superior sagittal sinus thrombosis would be less likely in this patient with recent stroke.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> New midbrain lesion is incorrect. The midbrain is supplied by the branches of the basilar artery, including the posterior cerebral artery, the superior cerebellar artery, and the posterior choroidal artery. This patient's initial presentation may have represented a right posterior cerebral artery infarct, given its supply to the occipital lobes, temporal lobes, parietal lobes, and thalamus, as well as the brainstem. While a lesion of the right midbrain could affect the cerebral peduncle and the oculomotor nerve, resulting in dilated right pupil with left-sided weakness, this would be much less likely than cerebral edema from his initial infarct resulting in uncal herniation, especially in the setting of obtundation.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> New pontine lesion is incorrect. The pons is supplied by branches of the basilar artery, including the pontine arteries, the anterior inferior cerebellar artery, and the superior cerebellar artery. Symptomatology of a stroke in this area depends on the blood vessel involved but may include truncal ataxia, diplopia, nystagmus, facial droop, decreased lacrimation and salivation, vertigo, and locked-in syndrome or coma. Unilateral pupillary dilation would not be expected.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Subarachnoid hemorrhage is incorrect. Subarachnoid hemorrhages are most often caused by rupture of a saccular aneurysm, though they also may occur secondary to head trauma, and typically present with a severe headache of precipitous onset, nausea, vomiting, meningism, coma, and potentially death. New-onset oculomotor paralysis should raise concern for the presence of a saccular aneurysm of the ipsilateral posterior communicating artery, which can compress the oculomotor nerve. However, a new-onset aneurysm is less likely in this patient than worsening cerebral edema leading to herniation.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis is incorrect. Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis most commonly occurs in patients with head trauma, intracranial infection, or a hypercoagulable state, such as pregnancy. While a rare occurrence, sagittal sinus thrombosis typically presents with headache, nausea, vomiting, seizures, and hemiparesis.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: For patients on hospice care, ensuring comfort is the foremost concern. The most appropriate next step is to give whatever dose is necessary; for this patient, relief of pain is the physician's primary responsibility.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> In the context of hospice care, the doctrine of double effect is a key ethical principle that helps physicians make decisions about administering treatments that relieve pain but might also have serious adverse effects.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> The doctrine of double effect is a key ethical principle in administering pain medications to hospice patients, where the primary goal is to relieve suffering. This principle allows for the use of medications that may have the secondary, unintended effect of hastening death, provided the intent is solely to alleviate pain. In hospice care, maintaining patient comfort and dignity is paramount, necessitating the careful administration of analgesics. For this patient on hospice care with exquisite pain due to multiple myeloma, the physician's main goal and responsibility to the patient is to ensure comfort. This holds true in this case even if it results in respiratory suppression, altered mental status, and even potentially death.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with terminal multiple myeloma, is unable to move, and is in extreme pain. She has been placed into 24-hour hospice care. In this setting, the physician's primary focus and goal for the patient is to ensure comfort, even if this results in hastening of death.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Attempt to use other methods of pain relief is incorrect. Attempting to use other methods of pain relief in a hospice patient experiencing extreme pain is inappropriate because it may delay effective symptom management, compromising the patient's quality of life.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Increase the dose as long as the Po2 and Pco2 remain within normal limits is incorrect. Increasing the dose only if Po2 and Pco2 remain within normal limits is not suitable, as it prioritizes physiologic parameters over the immediate need for effective pain control and comfort in hospice care.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Reduce the dose if the dose of medication adequate to relieve pain may hasten the patient's death is incorrect. Reducing the dose if it may hasten death is inappropriate under the doctrine of double effect, as the primary intention of hospice care is to alleviate suffering, not to prolong life at the expense of unbearable pain.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Reduce the dose to avoid the risk of addiction is incorrect. Reducing the dose to avoid the risk of addiction is not appropriate in hospice care, as the primary goal is to relieve pain and provide comfort, with addiction being a nonissue in end-of-life care.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has tabes dorsalis, which is a late manifestation of neurosyphilis characterized by sensory ataxia, visual disturbances, and lancinating pains. Penicillin is the primary treatment to halt progression and the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy for this patient.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Tabes dorsalis presents with progressive sensory ataxia, lancinating pains, and diminished deep tendon reflexes due to degeneration of the dorsal columns and roots of the spinal cord.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Tabes dorsalis is a slowly progressive neurologic condition resulting from the degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots of the spinal cord, primarily caused by untreated syphilis in its tertiary stage. This degeneration leads to the loss of proprioception, vibration sense, and deep tendon reflexes, manifesting clinically as ataxia, a broad-based gait, and a characteristic \"slapping\" foot pattern during walking. Patients often experience intense, stabbing pains because of the involvement of sensory nerve fibers, along with bladder disturbances and episodes of sudden and severe shooting pains. The condition is associated with Argyll Robertson pupils, which are small pupils that constrict poorly to light but respond normally to accommodation, reflecting a specific neurologic deficit. Diagnosis typically involves clinical evaluation, supported by serologic testing for syphilis and cerebrospinal fluid analysis, which may show increased protein concentrations and a positive VDRL test.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention of tabes dorsalis involves early detection and treatment of syphilis, particularly in the primary and secondary stages, to prevent progression to tertiary syphilis.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> The treatment for tabes dorsalis primarily focuses on antibiotic therapy with penicillin to address the underlying syphilis, along with symptomatic management of pain and neurologic deficits.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient is presenting with a recent history of increased falls and light intolerance, as well as a long history of difficulty walking, particularly at night. She has a history of multiple sexual partners and sexually transmitted infections. On physical examination, she shows Argyll Robertson pupils, which are characteristic of tertiary syphilis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Oral tetracycline is incorrect. Oral tetracycline is used in the treatment of sexually transmitted infections, such as chlamydia, and as an alternative regimen for syphilis in patients allergic to penicillin.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Oral tobramycin, rifampin, and isoniazid is incorrect. Rifampin and isoniazid are key components of the standard treatment regimen for tuberculosis, including central nervous system (CNS) tuberculosis, but they are generally not used in combination with tobramycin.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Intramuscular vitamin B12 (cyanocobalamin) is incorrect. Intramuscular vitamin B12 is administered for the treatment of vitamin B12 deficiency, which can lead to neurologic disorders, such as subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. However, this would not explain the patient's Argyll Robertson pupils.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Intravenous acyclovir is incorrect. Intravenous acyclovir is used for the treatment of severe herpes simplex, including herpes encephalitis (a CNS disorder).</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Intravenous ganciclovir is incorrect. Intravenous ganciclovir is used to treat cytomegalovirus infections, particularly cytomegalovirus retinitis in immunocompromised patients. It can also be used in the treatment of CNS involvement in cytomegalovirus infections.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Tuberous sclerosis is the most likely diagnosis in this patient experiencing new-onset seizures with facial angiofibromas, periungual fibromas, and ash-leaf spots. Most patients with tuberous sclerosis will develop seizures over their lifetime.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with tuberous sclerosis often present with seizures in addition to cutaneous findings including ash-leaf spots, facial angiofibromas, and periungual fibromas. In patients with new-onset seizures, intracranial imaging is recommended.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Tuberous sclerosis is a genetic condition associated with mutations of the TSC gene. As a result, patients develop hamartomas involving the skin, nervous system, heart, and kidneys. In addition to cutaneous findings, patients may develop renal angiomyolipomas, cardiac rhabdomyomas, and intracranial neoplasms including subependymal nodules, cortical tubers, astrocytomas, and intracranial calcifications. Abnormal electrical activity from intracranial hamartomas may predispose these patients to seizures.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no effective preventive treatment for tuberous sclerosis.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Patients with tuberous sclerosis require anticonvulsant medication for seizures and may require surgical resection of rapidly growing or symptomatic tumors.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In patients with cutaneous hypopigmented lesions, facial angiofibromas, periungual fibromas, and seizures, tuberous sclerosis represents the most likely diagnosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Congenital syphilis is incorrect. Congenital syphilis is associated with maternal-fetal transmission of Treponema pallidum. Congenital syphilis may manifest with early findings including secondary syphilis lesions, hepatosplenomegaly, condyloma lata, and snuffles. Late manifestations include deafness, frontal bossing, dental abnormalities, long bone deformity, nasal bone deformity, and tabes dorsalis. Congenital syphilis may be prevented through prenatal screening and affected individuals should be treated with penicillin.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Neurofibromatosis is incorrect. Neurofibromatosis is an autosomal dominant disease characterized by the development of neurofibromas, plexiform neurofibromas, hyperpigmented maculae, axillary freckling, optic nerve gliomas, iris hamartomas (Lisch nodules), and bony abnormalities such as long bone dysplasia and sphenoid wing dysplasia.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Sturge-Weber syndrome is incorrect. Sturge-Weber syndrome is a neurocutaneous disorder caused by mutation in the GNAQ gene that presents with the proliferation of capillaries, seen on examination as nevus flammeus. Patients additionally develop angiomatosis of the leptomeninges, which may lead to seizures, intellectual disabilities, and focal neurologic deficits.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> von Hippel disease is incorrect. von Hippel disease is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous disorder that presents with multiple benign and malignant tumors. Many tumors originate from the vasculature, including hemangioblastomas of the retina and central nervous system, and angiomatosis of the skin and mucosa. Tumors of the kidney and pancreas also occur.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient with small cell lung cancer presents with temporal lobe epileptiform activity, roving eye movements, and positive Babinski sign. This is consistent with paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis, an immune-mediated complication of malignancy.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis presents with rapidly progressive memory loss, seizures, and new psychiatric symptoms. Paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis stems from an immunologic response against malignancy and can be seen in certain malignancies such as small cell lung cancer. The immune response leads to inflammation of the limbic system.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis is a diagnosis of exclusion; however, positive findings, if present, include cerebrospinal fluid showing inflammation, MRI with abnormalities in the temporal lobe, and EEG findings with epileptic activity in the temporal lobe, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention of malignancy is the mainstay of preventing paraneoplastic syndrome. There are no established preventive measures for paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Addressing the underlying malignancy with treatment such as chemotherapy or radiation therapy is the mainstay of treatment.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> There is a wide differential diagnosis for paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis, which includes infectious encephalitis, autoimmune disease, toxic-metabolic encephalopathy, and primary central nervous system malignancies. In this patient with temporal lobe seizures and recent diagnosis of small cell lung cancer, paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis is the most likely diagnosis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Medication adverse effect is incorrect. Cefepime, not ceftriaxone, is known to cause neurotoxicity.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Metastatic disease is not the best choice. Metastatic disease to the brain could cause seizure, as seen in this patient; however, a metastatic lesion would generally be seen on MRI. The lack of a lesion on MRI makes this answer less likely.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Prion disease is incorrect. Prion diseases are rapidly progressive neurodegenerative disorders, which can present with neurologic symptoms like those seen in this patient. However, prion diseases classically have high signal intensity and cortical ribboning seen on MRI, along with increased 14-3-3 protein concentrations on cerebrospinal fluid analysis.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Viral infection is incorrect. Viral infection, such as viral encephalitis, would be more likely to present with acute onset of fever, headache, and altered mental status. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis may be similar between viral encephalitis and paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis, but this patient's temporal lobe epileptiform activity and recent diagnosis of small cell lung cancer make paraneoplastic limbic encephalitis a more likely answer.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most appropriate next step for this patient is oral nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory therapy. NSAIDs are first-line pharmacologic therapy for mechanical low back pain in the absence of neurologic deficits.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Mechanical low back pain can present with symptoms isolated to the low back, axial back pain, or can have a radicular component to either leg. Some patients, if compression of neurologic structures is severe enough, can have weakness, areflexia, and sensory abnormalities.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> A patient presenting with low back pain radiating down one leg is often experiencing symptoms of lumbar radiculopathy, typically caused by a herniated disc compressing a nerve root, such as the L5 or S1 root. The herniation occurs when the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral disc protrudes through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, exerting pressure on the adjacent nerve root and causing sharp, shooting pain along the nerve's dermatome. This condition can also be associated with lumbar spinal stenosis, where narrowing of the spinal canal or foramina leads to nerve compression, or spondylolisthesis, where a vertebra slips forward over the one below it, causing similar symptoms. In some cases, piriformis syndrome may be the culprit, where the piriformis muscle irritates the sciatic nerve, mimicking radicular pain patterns. Diagnosis typically involves a combination of clinical examination, imaging studies such as MRI to visualize the soft tissues, and sometimes electrodiagnostic tests to differentiate between radiculopathy and other neuropathic conditions.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> To prevent low back pain, individuals should maintain a healthy weight, practice proper body mechanics, and engage in regular core-strengthening exercises to support spinal stability.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment for low back pain with radiating leg pain often includes a combination of rest, physical therapy, and medications such as NSAIDs to decrease inflammation, with more severe cases potentially requiring interventions like epidural corticosteroid injections or surgical evaluation.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with low back pain and mild radiculopathy as evidenced by the positive straight-leg raise test and pain in her leg. In the absence of hard neurologic deficits such as weakness, paresthesias, or areflexia, conservative management is appropriate. The Strategies for Prescribing Analgesics Comparative Effectiveness (SPACE) study from 2018 showed that opioid medications were nonsuperior to nonopioid medications at improving pain-related function at 12 months. Thus, NSAIDs are a more appropriate first option for this patient.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Corticosteroid injection therapy is incorrect. A corticosteroid injection is beneficial in decreasing inflammation and pain in patients for whom more conservative therapies have failed. This is not indicated at this time.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Lumbar laminectomy is incorrect. Lumbar laminectomy should be pursued in patients with symptomatic spinal stenosis with neurologic deficits or intractable pain. Conservative treatment is more appropriate at this time.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Oral muscle relaxant therapy is incorrect. Muscle relaxants are a fair consideration in the treatment of muscular low back pain, though they should be used after conservative treatments such as NSAIDs.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Oral opioid therapy is incorrect. Opioid medications have been shown to be nonsuperior to nonopioid medications for the improvement of pain-related function. Conservative treatment with NSAIDs is more appropriate for this patient.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most appropriate initial step for this patient is canalith repositioning. The Epley maneuver is a highly effective treatment for benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV), designed to reposition displaced otoliths within the semicircular canals of the inner ear to alleviate symptoms.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with BPPV typically present with brief episodes of intense vertigo triggered by specific changes in head position, such as rolling over in bed or looking up, without significant hearing loss or tinnitus.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> BPPV is a common vestibular disorder characterized by brief episodes of vertigo that are triggered by changes in head position. This condition is caused by the displacement of otoliths, small calcium carbonate crystals, into the semicircular canals of the inner ear, leading to inappropriate stimulation of the vestibular system. Patients with BPPV often experience intense dizziness, usually lasting less than 1 minute, when performing actions such as rolling over in bed, bending over, or looking up. Although nausea can accompany these vertiginous episodes, significant hearing loss and tinnitus are not typical features of BPPV. The diagnosis is primarily clinical, confirmed by the Dix-Hallpike maneuver, which induces characteristic nystagmus and vertigo in affected individuals.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> While there is no guaranteed way to prevent BPPV, avoiding rapid head movements and maintaining general vestibular health can potentially decrease the risk for recurrence.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> The primary treatment for BPPV involves canalith repositioning maneuvers, such as the Epley maneuver, which aim to move the dislodged otoliths out of the semicircular canals.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The Epley maneuver is the best first step in managing BPPV because it is highly effective, is noninvasive, provides rapid symptom relief, and can be easily performed without special equipment.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Clopidogrel is incorrect. Clopidogrel is not the appropriate first step for BPPV as it is primarily used to decrease the risk for stroke and myocardial infarction by preventing blood clots.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Diazepam is incorrect. Diazepam is not the best choice for BPPV because it acts as a sedative, which might dull the symptoms but does not address the problem.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Low-sodium diet is incorrect. Decreasing sodium intake is not effective for BPPV since this dietary change is more applicable to conditions like Meniere disease, where fluid balance in the ear is a concern.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Meclizine is incorrect. Using meclizine for BPPV is not ideal because it mainly offers temporary relief from dizziness without resolving the positional cause.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This young patient presents with a history of frontal headaches, vision changes, and a decrease in his rate of growth. This presentation is suggestive of craniopharyngioma or another pituitary lesion compressing the optic chiasm.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Craniopharyngioma is a benign intracranial tumor that arises from remnants of Rathke pouch. Craniopharyngioma typically develops near the optic chiasm in the sellar region of the brain and presents with symptoms of increased intracranial pressure, visual impairment, and endocrinopathy.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Sellar lesions that compress the optic chiasm can all cause bitemporal hemianopsia; if sellar lesions are either functional or impairing pituitary function, growth abnormalities, developmental delay, sexual dysfunction, and thyroid dysfunction can occur. MRI, the primary modality of diagnosis of suspected sellar lesions, is obtained after a clinical presentation suggests the diagnosis.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is genetic predisposal to sellar lesions, but no specific preventive therapy exists.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Craniopharyngiomas are classically treated with surgical resection and complete removal of the tumor.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Signs of increased intracranial pressure, such as positional headaches with visual disturbance (bitemporal hemianopsia), narrow the differential diagnosis to a space-occupying lesion in the central nervous system such as a tumor.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Occipital lobe is incorrect. Occipital lobe lesions may present with headaches and visual field defects; however, the growth failure seen in this patient is consistent with a lesion in the sellar region compressing both the optic chiasm and the pituitary gland.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Optic nerve is incorrect. Optic nerve lesions present with decreased visual acuity and are classically a unilateral deficit. It would be atypical for a craniopharyngioma to compress the optic nerve.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Optic radiations is incorrect. Optic radiations are a part of the visual pathway that connect the lateral geniculate nucleus to the visual cortex of the occipital lobe. Damage to optic radiations, depending on anatomic location, can present with quadrantanopia.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Optic tract is incorrect. Optic tract lesions present with homonymous hemianopsia and may be caused by craniopharyngioma. However, in this patient with decreased growth, compression of the pituitary gland is also likely occurring, which makes the lesion more likely to be in the sellar region of the brain.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient, with profound hypertension and symptoms including headaches, blurred vision, nausea, vomiting, somnolence, papilledema, retinal hemorrhages, and generalized hyperreflexia, has acute hypertensive encephalopathy. Treatment includes emergent intravenous administration of antihypertensive agents.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Hypertensive encephalopathy may present with headache, vomiting, altered mental status, and seizures, and may progress to obtundation and coma. Optic disc edema, as well as retinal hemorrhages, may be present.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Hypertensive encephalopathy may result from acute and severe increases in blood pressure due to disruptions in cerebral autoregulation, resulting in cerebral edema. Visual symptoms may result from cortical hypertension or hypertensive retinopathy. Other signs of end-organ damage, such as kidney injury with hematuria, may be present. Hypertensive encephalopathy is a diagnosis of exclusion relying on an improvement in symptoms with a decrease in blood pressure.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention includes appropriate treatment of hypertension, including compliance with prescribed oral medications, plus avoidance of agents that can abruptly increase blood pressure (eg, cocaine, amphetamines).</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment includes decreasing blood pressure while avoiding excessively rapid drops in blood pressure, which can in turn cause organ ischemia. In general, a decrease in the mean arterial pressure of 25% within the first hour is appropriate, with gradual correction over the next 24 to 48 hours.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Administration of additional intravenous volume, whether normal saline or colloid, would not improve this patient's presentation and may lead to worsening hypertension, volume overload, and pulmonary edema. While he is increasingly somnolent, he still arouses to verbal stimuli, making clearance of the airway and oxygen administration unnecessary. A lumbar puncture should be avoided in patients with evidence of increased intracranial pressure, such as papilledema, and while control of this increased pressure would be appropriate, it is more pertinent to first administer antihypertensive agents to decrease blood pressure.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Administration of normal saline maintenance fluids is incorrect. Normal saline is often administered in patients with dehydration, hypovolemia, or hyponatremia, or in those who are unable to take oral hydration (eg, prior to a procedure, intractable nausea and vomiting). Additionally, administration of additional volume in profound hypertension may lead to worsening hypertension, volume overload, and pulmonary edema.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Clearance of the airway and administration of oxygen by face mask is incorrect. As for all patients, initial evaluation of this patient should include an assessment of the airway, breathing, and circulation. Given that he is responsive to verbal stimuli, there is evidence that his airway and breathing are intact, making clearance of the airway unnecessary.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Colloid expansion of intravascular volume is incorrect. Colloid expansion with albumin or blood is often used in patients with hemorrhagic or hypovolemic shock. This patient does not have evidence of volume depletion and rapid expansion of his intravascular volume may lead to volume overload and pulmonary edema.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Control of increased intracranial pressure is incorrect. Control of increased intracranial pressure can be accomplished with sedation, pain control, hypertonic saline or mannitol administration, acute hyperventilation, maintenance of normothermia, and drainage of cerebrospinal fluid. However, while hypertensive encephalopathy is associated with increased intracranial pressure, the most appropriate management for this patient is decreasing systemic blood pressure with intravenous antihypertensive agents.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Lumbar puncture is incorrect. In cases of increased intracranial pressure as evidenced by papilledema, lumbar puncture may increase the risk for herniation and is contraindicated.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is conversion disorder, also known as functional neurological symptom disorder. Functional neurological symptom disorder involves neurologic symptoms, such as sensory or motor dysfunction, that are not fully explained by objective findings on physical examination or imaging.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> This patient's motor weakness and normal reflexes, as well as her hemisensory deficit that includes the face and scalp, are inconsistent with structural neurologic disease. Though the symptoms can disrupt daily life, patients with functional neurological symptom disorder may or may not be preoccupied with their symptoms.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Functional neurological symptom disorder is commonly idiopathic, though for some patients it is postulated to represent the subconscious conversion of intolerable emotional distress into neurologic dysfunction. Psychiatric comorbidities, such as depression, are common but not present in all patients.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> As the cause of functional neurological symptom disorder is not fully understood, there are no universal prevention strategies.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Physicians should conduct a thorough history and physical examination and obtain laboratory and imaging studies to evaluate for medical or neurologic disease that may explain or partially explain the patient's symptoms. Treatment of functional neurological symptom disorder involves education about the diagnosis, physical therapy, and cognitive behavioral therapy.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Cerebral infarction (stroke), which may similarly present with the sudden onset of new neurologic symptoms, should be excluded. However, in strokes, neurologic deficits typically localize to a central nervous system region, whereas in functional neurological symptom disorder, neurologic deficits do not localize (they are inconsistent with structural neurologic disease).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Complicated migraine is incorrect. Migraine is a primary headache associated with nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia. Complicated migraine may present with vision changes, weakness, or sensory deficits. However, this patient does not currently have a headache.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lacunar infarct is incorrect. Lacunar infarction describes ischemia to the deep cerebral white matter, pons, or basal ganglia, which is not transient. A lacunar infarction may present with purely motor symptoms, purely sensory symptoms, ataxic hemiparesis, or dysarthria. This patient instead presents with a combination of motor and sensory symptoms that does not localize to the deep cerebral white matter, pons, or basal ganglia. Also, her MRI of the brain did not show infarcts.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Middle cerebral artery occlusion is incorrect. Occlusion of the middle cerebral artery leads to contralateral hemiparesis and contralateral sensory deficit of the face and upper extremities. This patient's lower extremity symptoms are inconsistent with a middle cerebral artery occlusion. Also, her MRI of the brain did not show infarcts.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Venous sinus thrombosis is incorrect. Venous sinus thrombosis may be induced by an infection of facial skin, orbit, or nasal sinuses, which causes a septic thrombus to form, or occur spontaneously in the setting of hypercoagulable states. Presenting symptoms commonly include acute onset of headache, orbital pain, edema, visual disturbance, and mental status changes. This patient does not present with any of these symptoms.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: It is most appropriate to assess this patient's ability to transfer. The ability to safely transfer from one surface to another is a crucial determinant in assessing whether a patient with multiple sclerosis (MS) requires a higher level of care, as it directly impacts their risk for falls and overall independence.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Evaluating whether a patient with MS requires a higher level of care involves assessing cognitive and physical function, social support systems, and the availability of adaptive resources.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Cognitive function is a critical consideration for patients with MS, as impairments can hinder decision-making and the safe management of daily activities. Assessing mobility and physical function is essential, since difficulties in these areas might increase the risk for falls or injuries, suggesting a need for more support. The strength of social support systems, including family and community resources, can influence whether a patient requires additional care by providing necessary assistance and companionship. Lastly, the availability and accessibility of adaptive equipment and home modifications can decrease the need for a higher level of care by helping patients overcome physical limitations.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient with MS had a recent exacerbation, and she is now required to use a walker for ambulation. Aside from this, she is cognitively intact, has good social support, and can feed herself. At this time, the most important assessment for this patient is an assessment of her ability to transfer safely, so that she can perform activities of daily living, such as bathing, getting dressed, and hygiene.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Driving is incorrect. Driving is not the most pressing issue at this time because the patient receives regular assistance from a neighbor who visits daily, decreasing the immediate necessity for the patient to drive independently on a regular basis.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Managing finances is incorrect. Managing finances is not the most appropriate concern because the patient's cognitive evaluation is normal, suggesting she can handle financial tasks independently despite physical limitations.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Managing medications is incorrect. Managing medications is not the primary focus because the patient's cognitive function is intact, indicating she can likely manage her medication regimen, which currently only includes one medication.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Performing housework is incorrect. Performing housework is less relevant at this time because the assessment of physical function, particularly the ability to transfer, is more directly related to immediate safety and risk for falls.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Preparing meals is incorrect. Preparing meals is not the most pressing issue because the patient has a neighbor who delivers groceries and visits daily, providing support that mitigates any immediate concern.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lyme disease is a tick-borne disease, caused by Borrelia burgdorferi, endemic in the Northeastern United States. Erythema chronicum migrans, an expanding rash with a central ring-like clearing, is often the first manifestation. Neurologic manifestations include lymphocytic meningitis, cranial neuritis (ie, facial nerve palsy, which may be bilateral, as in this patient), and radiculoneuritis (ie, inflammation of spinal nerves). Diagnosis can be determined by serological and/or cerebrospinal fluid studies for antibodies to B burgdorferi, such as IgG and IgM Lyme antibody testing.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Lyme disease is a tick-borne disease endemic to the Northeastern United States. In its early stages, Lyme disease can cause erythema chronicum migrans, which is an expanding rash that may have a central ring-like clearing. It can also be associated with a flu-like illness. Neurologic manifestations include lymphocytic meningitis, cranial neuritis (ie, facial nerve palsy, which may be bilateral, as in this patient), and radiculoneuritis (ie, inflammation of spinal nerves). Later stages of Lyme disease can be characterized by carditis, atrioventricular heart block, migratory arthralgias, and if left untreated, encephalopathy and chronic arthritis.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Lyme disease is a tick-borne disease caused by the spirochete B burgdorferi, which is endemic to the Northeastern United States and commonly transmitted by the Ixodes scapularis tick. Diagnosis involves serological testing for IgM and/or IgG Lyme antibodies. These laboratory studies should also be performed on the cerebrospinal fluid of patients in endemic areas with neurologic manifestations.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Patients who live in endemic areas should take measures to decrease risk for tick bites, such as wearing long pants and applying permethrin to clothing. They should also be advised to conduct \"tick checks\" after spending time outside. If ticks are found, they should be removed promptly, and the patient should self-monitor for symptoms of Lyme disease.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Lyme disease is treated with doxycycline or amoxicillin in the early stages, with amoxicillin preferred in pediatric patients. Parenteral ceftriaxone is indicated for late-stage or disseminated cases, including cases that affect the heart or central nervous system.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In this patient with cranial nerve dysfunction, residence in an endemic area, and recent outdoor activities, a high index of suspicion for Lyme disease should be conferred.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Culture for Borrelia burgdorferi is incorrect. Antibody assays are the gold standard for the diagnosis of Lyme disease.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Oligoclonal bands is incorrect. Increased oligoclonal bands in cerebrospinal fluid is characteristic of multiple sclerosis. Patients can develop a variety of sensory and motor symptoms, often distributed over time, but characteristically present with internuclear ophthalmoplegia. Isolated facial diplegia would be an uncommon manifestation of multiple sclerosis, as would presentation at age 17 years.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Cytomorphology of lymphocytes is incorrect. This is a component of the workup of meningitis, with abundant neutrophils suggesting bacterial meningitis, and abundant lymphocytes and monocytes suggesting viral meningitis. Isolated facial diplegia is more likely to be a neurologic manifestation of Lyme disease, rather than meningitis.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Polymerase chain reaction for Borrelia burgdorferi is incorrect. Antibody assays are the gold standard for the diagnosis of Lyme disease.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has Weber syndrome, which is characterized by the combination of ipsilateral oculomotor paralysis and contralateral hemiparesis due to a midbrain lesion.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with Weber syndrome typically present with ptosis, mydriasis (pupil dilation), and strabismus (specifically a \"down and out\" ocular position on the affected side due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles), alongside weakness on the opposite side of the body.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Weber syndrome, also known as superior alternating hemiplegia, is a midbrain stroke syndrome characterized by ipsilateral oculomotor paralysis and contralateral hemiparesis or hemiplegia. This condition results from a lesion in the midbrain tegmentum, typically due to infarction of the penetrating branches of the posterior cerebral artery or a paramedian basilar artery branch, affecting the cerebral peduncle and the oculomotor fascicles. The contralateral hemiparesis arises from damage to the corticospinal tract within the cerebral peduncle, affecting voluntary motor control on the opposite side of the body. Additional clinical features may include contralateral lower facial weakness and tongue deviation due to corticobulbar tract involvement, and in some cases, there may be sensory deficits.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention of Weber syndrome primarily involves managing risk factors for stroke, such as hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and hyperlipidemia, to decrease the likelihood of vascular events affecting the midbrain.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment focuses on addressing the underlying cause of the stroke, which may include antiplatelet therapy, anticoagulation, or surgical intervention, alongside supportive care and rehabilitation to manage symptoms and improve function.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with neurologic deficits on awakening in the morning. She presents with ptosis, oculomotor paralysis, mydriasis, contralateral weakness, and present Babinski sign. All these signs are consistent with brain stem bleeding, with localization to the left midbrain due to the left-sided oculomotor paralysis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F.</p><p><b>Choice A and Choice B:</b> Left internal capsule and right internal capsule are incorrect. A stroke in the internal capsule often results in pure motor hemiparesis or hemiplegia affecting the contralateral side of the body. Oculomotor paralysis would not be expected.</p><p><b>Choice C and Choice D:</b> Left medulla and right medulla are incorrect. A stroke in the medulla can result in Wallenberg syndrome (also known as lateral medullary syndrome), which is characterized by symptoms such as ipsilateral ataxia, Horner syndrome, loss of pain and temperature sensation on the ipsilateral face and contralateral body, dysphagia, and vertigo. This patient's signs and symptoms are most consistent with Weber syndrome.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Right midbrain is incorrect. While a right midbrain lesion can lead to Weber syndrome, this patient would present with right oculomotor paralysis and left hemiparesis.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has dementia, Alzheimer type (AD), which is the most prevalent dementia, presenting with progressive cognitive decline that culminates in the inability to perform basic activities of daily living. AD is a clinical diagnosis and requires no additional testing.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> AD is the most prevalent dementia, presenting with progressive cognitive decline that begins with short-term memory impairment and decline in visuospatial abilities and progresses to apraxia, language abnormalities, and dysexecutive syndrome (as seen in this patient, who draws the face of a clock inaccurately). The disease culminates in behavioral and personality changes which prevent the patient from performing basic activities of daily living, such as bathing. AD is a clinical diagnosis that does not require specific testing.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> AD results from decreased cholinergic signaling and is treated with acetylcholinesterase inhibitors. Cholinergic neurons, which primarily originate from the locus coeruleus and basal forebrain and project widely to areas including the cortex, mediate attention, learning, and memory.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prevention primarily involves interventions to decrease cardiovascular risk, such as maintaining a healthy diet, exercising, and avoiding smoking.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Donepezil and other cholinesterase inhibitors noncompetitively and reversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase activity and thereby increase the amount of synaptic acetylcholine available for neurotransmission. Though donepezil may slow the rate of cognitive decline and modestly improve functionality, donepezil and the other cholinesterase inhibitors are not curative.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Though not required, neuropsychological testing can be helpful to confirm the diagnosis of AD and establish a cognitive baseline.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Determination of apolipoprotein E haplotype is incorrect. Though genetic testing for apolipoprotein E haplotype may guide selection of amyloid targeted therapy, genetic testing does not significantly add to AD risk stratification as false positives and negatives are common.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Measurement of serum ceruloplasmin concentration is incorrect. Serum ceruloplasmin concentrations are used in the diagnosis of hepatolenticular degeneration (Wilson disease), not AD.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> EEG is incorrect. EEG may assist in diagnosing delirium or cognitive impairment when the cause of mental status change is unclear. However, the diagnosis of delirium or cognitive impairment can frequently be made clinically.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Measurement of cerebrospinal fluid protein concentration is incorrect. Though cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers have received increased interest in predicting or confirming AD, these biomarkers are not used uniformly, and a clinical diagnosis suffices.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has transient global amnesia (TGA), which presents with sudden and transient anterograde and retrograde amnesia in older adult patients. TGA is a self-limited, idiopathic condition.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> TGA typically presents with the acute onset of hours of anterograde and retrograde amnesia in patients aged between 50 and 80 years. Patients may be disoriented to external facets, but self-awareness usually remains intact. TGA is a clinical diagnosis.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> TGA is commonly a benign and self-limited condition. The cause is unclear but may involve cortical depression or metabolic insults to the hippocampus, a brain area vulnerable to cytotoxicity.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Given its unclear cause, there are no universal strategies for preventing TGA.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> As TGA is benign and self-limited, management involves reassurance.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> A stroke of the posterior circulation, which can present with memory deficits and focal neurologic deficits, should be excluded. Seizures with impaired awareness should also be excluded. Delirium is on the differential, though confusion is more pervasive and can involve self-awareness deficits.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Benzodiazepine intoxication is incorrect. Patients with benzodiazepine intoxication typically present with central nervous system depression, and in severe cases, may present with memory deficits. However, this patient is alert and without central nervous system depression.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Dementia, Alzheimer type, is incorrect. Cognitive deficits are insidious in onset and irreversible in dementia, Alzheimer type.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Multi-infarct (vascular) dementia is incorrect. Patients with vascular dementia commonly present with focal neurologic deficits, and cognitive deficits are typically irreversible.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is incorrect. Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is an irreversible condition that features anterograde and retrograde amnesia along with the consequent tendency to confabulate (verbalize false stories or memories). This patient's amnesia is transient and reversible, making TGA the most likely diagnosis.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has common peroneal neuropathy, which often results from compression of the common fibular (peroneal) nerve as it courses around the fibular head, leading to footdrop, and is frequently associated with prolonged immobility or improper positioning during surgical procedures.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Patients with common peroneal neuropathy typically present with footdrop, characterized by weakness in dorsiflexion, and may experience sensory deficits, such as numbness or tingling along the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Common peroneal neuropathy is frequently attributed to compression or entrapment of the common peroneal nerve as it courses around the fibular head, but it can also occur due to prolonged immobility or specific positioning during extended medical procedures, such as surgeries requiring mechanical ventilation and sedation. Clinically, patients may report sensory disturbances, including numbness or paresthesia, along the anterolateral aspect of the leg and the dorsum of the foot. Diagnostic evaluation involves a thorough neurologic examination complemented by electrodiagnostic studies, such as nerve conduction velocity tests and electromyography, to assess the extent of nerve damage and localize the site of injury. Management strategies include physical therapy to strengthen affected muscles, the use of ankle-foot orthoses to prevent footdrop and addressing any modifiable risk factors or underlying conditions to prevent further nerve compression or injury.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with dorsiflexion and eversion weakness of the feet following bypass surgery and prolonged hospital stay. He has decreased sensation over the lateral legs and a negative straight leg raise, which are all consistent with common peroneal neuropathy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cauda equina is incorrect. Compression of the cauda equina typically presents with low back pain, bilateral lower extremity weakness, saddle anesthesia, and bowel or bladder dysfunction, necessitating urgent surgical evaluation.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lumbosacral plexus is incorrect. Compression of the lumbosacral plexus often results in a combination of motor and sensory deficits in the lower extremities, including weakness, numbness, and radiating pain that can affect the hip, thigh, and leg. Symptoms would not likely be isolated to footdrop.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Sciatic nerves is incorrect. Compression of the sciatic nerves usually presents with sharp, shooting pain that radiates from the lower back down through the buttock and posterior leg, accompanied by potential weakness or numbness in the affected areas.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Tibial nerves is incorrect. Compression of the tibial nerves often leads to sensory loss in the sole of the foot and weakness in plantar and toe flexion, rather than dorsiflexion weakness as seen in this patient.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient is most likely experiencing migraines. Migraines are typically unilateral, pulsating headaches that are associated with nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia, and may present with visual or sensory auras.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Migraine is a relatively common condition that presents with recurrent primary headaches. Some patients experience an aura before or during the headache, which may present with vision loss followed by the perception of scintillating shapes or lines and/or paresthesia.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Migraines are postulated to arise from deficient serotonin inhibition of descending pain pathways. Migraine triggers may include stress, menstruation, weather changes, fasting, and alcohol. There is no specific diagnostic test for migraines; the diagnosis is clinical. Laboratory or imaging studies, if obtained, will either be within normal limits or indicate a secondary, indirect triggering condition.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Migraines may be prevented by avoiding identified migraine triggers.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Acute migraine treatment includes triptans (serotonin agonists), calcitonin gene-related peptide receptor antagonists, dopamine antagonists, antiemetics, acetaminophen, and/or NSAIDs. For patients with disabling or frequent migraines, prophylactic treatment is recommended and may include β-adrenergic blockers, antidepressants such as tricyclic antidepressants and serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors, as well as anticonvulsants.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The patient presents with unilateral headaches with associated visual aura, which is a constellation of symptoms unique to migraine headaches.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, and G.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Adjustment disorder is not the most likely diagnosis. Adjustment disorder refers to the onset of an emotional disturbance within 3 months of an identifiable stressor. The emotional disturbance manifests as marked distress out of proportion to the stressor causing a significant impairment in daily school, work, or social functioning.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Bipolar disorder is incorrect. Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder that features acute episodes of mania and may include episodes of depression. Acute mania typically manifests with euphoric or irritable mood, increased energy, a decreased need for sleep, increased goal-directed activity, rapid speech and thinking, irresponsible behaviors, and grandiose thinking.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Chronic fatigue syndrome is not the most likely diagnosis. Chronic fatigue syndrome is characterized by excessive fatigue and associated symptoms for at least 6 months. Associated symptoms may include neurocognitive impairment, orthostatic intolerance, sleep disturbances, and psychiatric disturbances.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Dysthymic disorder is incorrect. Dysthymic disorder, or persistent depressive disorder, refers to at least three depression symptoms (including depressed mood during more days than not) that have endured for at least 2 years. The patient in this case is more likely experiencing migraines.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Fibromyalgia is not the most likely diagnosis. Fibromyalgia is characterized by diffuse musculoskeletal pain with associated cognitive disturbances, fatigue, or psychiatric disturbances. Migraine is the most likely diagnosis in this case.</p><p><b>Choice G:</b> Normal development is not the most likely diagnosis. The patient in this case is presenting with normal development. However, the patient is experiencing intermittent unilateral headaches with visual aura that interfere with her ability to attend school, which is not a feature of normal development and is consistent with migraine.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS). NMS presents with altered mental status, muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic dysfunction.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> NMS is a life-threatening condition associated with the antagonism of dopamine that presents with altered mental status, muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic dysfunction. Common causative agents are antipsychotics (especially high-potency antipsychotics such as haloperidol) and antidopaminergic nausea medications.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Muscle rigidity results from dopamine antagonism in the nigrostriatal pathway, while autonomic dysfunction and hyperthermia may result from dopamine antagonism in the hypothalamus. Characteristic laboratory abnormalities include leukocytosis and increased serum creatine kinase, lactate dehydrogenase, and alkaline phosphatase (due to prolonged muscle rigidity and consequent myolysis).</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> NMS can be prevented by avoiding high doses of antidopaminergic medication.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment is supportive and includes discontinuation of the offending medication, hydration, cooling blankets, and benzodiazepines if needed for agitation. Dantrolene or bromocriptine are potentially beneficial.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Malignant catatonia presents similarly to NMS, though it is not typically triggered by antidopaminergic medication.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Bacterial meningitis is incorrect. This patient lacks characteristic physical examination signs of bacterial meningitis such as a stiff neck and pain with knee extension. As well, autonomic instability and muscle rigidity are more consistent with NMS. This patient also presents with the NMS risk factor of recent potent antipsychotic use.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Heat stroke is incorrect. Heat stroke presents with hyperthermia, tachycardia, tachypnea, and altered mental status. However, other examination findings commonly include flushed, dry skin and manifestations of coagulopathy such as ecchymosis. As well, increased muscle tone would be atypical.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Sepsis is incorrect. Though sepsis can present with delirium along with fever and tachycardia, autonomic instability and muscle rigidity are less common. As well, this patient presents with the NMS risk factor of recent potent antipsychotic use.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Thyrotoxicosis is incorrect. This patient presents with a slightly decreased TSH, though thyrotoxicosis is associated with low-to-undetectable TSH. Thyrotoxicosis presents with altered mental status, fever, tachycardia, signs of cardiac failure, and impaired liver function in extreme cases. Autonomic instability and muscle rigidity are less common. As well, this patient presents with the NMS risk factor of recent potent antipsychotic use.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presents with delirium and confusion secondary to toxic-metabolic encephalopathy. Physical examination findings in toxic-metabolic encephalopathy may include asterixis and tremor (as seen in this patient), as well as myoclonus.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Delirium is a clinical syndrome characterized by an acute-onset alteration of consciousness and cognition, with symptoms that wax and wane throughout the day. Toxic-metabolic encephalopathy, or brain dysfunction secondary to medications or the accumulation of metabolites, is a common cause of delirium. Via unclear mechanisms, toxic-metabolic encephalopathy can lead to asterixis, or flapping tremor, which constitutes dysfunction in sustained muscle contraction.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> In older patients, especially those with underlying dementia, systemic infection (eg, pneumonia) can rapidly lead to the development of toxic delirium. All patients with delirium should be evaluated for evidence of systemic infection with urinalysis and culture, chest x-ray, and occasionally with blood cultures if indicated. Other common causes of delirium include intoxication and adverse effects from medications such as anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, or opiates.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Delirium can be prevented by mitigating risk factors including immobility and sensory impairment. Hospitalized patients should be regularly oriented and stimulated during the daytime, maintain normal sleep-wake cycles, and avoid deliriogenic medication.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of delirium should focus on treatment of the underlying condition (eg, antibiotics for pneumonia).</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Hepatic encephalopathy secondary to liver failure is associated with asterixis. However, this patient does not have risk factors for hepatic encephalopathy and instead has pneumonia, an acute precipitating factor for toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Acute liver failure is incorrect. While hepatic encephalopathy secondary to liver failure is associated with asterixis and altered mental status, this patient does not present with symptoms characteristic of acute liver failure such as right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, and nausea, among others. This patient has pneumonia, an acute precipitating factor for toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Hypothyroidism is incorrect. Though severe hypothyroidism can lead to delirium (eg, in myxedema coma) and is sometimes associated with asterixis, symptom onset would be more insidious than in toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Meningitis is incorrect. The patient lacks characteristic physical examination signs of meningitis such as a stiff neck and pain with knee extension. The most likely cause of the change in this patient's mental status is toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Stroke is incorrect. This patient does not show focal neurologic deficits as expected in a stroke; she has a bilateral tremor, which does not localize to a specific brain lesion. The most likely cause of the change in this patient's mental status is toxic-metabolic encephalopathy.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient with a 6-month history of uncontrollably falling asleep several times daily has narcolepsy. Narcolepsy is a chronic sleep disorder caused by deficient orexin secretion, leading to the intrusion of sleep phenomena (eg, sleepiness, cataplexy, hypnagogic hallucinations, sleep paralysis) into times of wakefulness. Modafinil, which is thought to increase orexin secretion, is the first-line medication for patients with narcolepsy/severe daytime sleepiness.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Narcolepsy is a chronic sleep disorder characterized by daytime sleepiness, cataplexy, hypnagogic hallucinations, and sleep paralysis. Clinical diagnosis should be confirmed through polysomnography (to confirm the rapid transition into REM sleep and rule out other causes of daytime sleepiness) and a multiple sleep latency test (to confirm decreased sleep latency). First-line pharmacotherapy is modafinil.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Narcolepsy arises from deficient production of orexins in the hypothalamus, which may result from genetic or autoimmune causes. Orexins typically induce the secretion of monoamines such as norepinephrine throughout the brain to promote wakefulness. As a result of orexin deficiency, patients suddenly and inappropriately transition into sleep during the day. Further, REM sleep features (eg, cataplexy, hypnagogic hallucinations, sleep paralysis) occur during wakefulness. Cataplexy refers to a sudden loss of muscle tone and usually occurs in response to positive emotion.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There is no universal prevention strategy for narcolepsy.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment includes a regular sleep schedule, avoidance of daytime naps, and, in patients with severe daytime sleepiness, modafinil therapy. Modafinil has an unclear mechanism of action that involves increasing orexin and monoamine availability.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Methylphenidate is a stimulant medication that can also be used to promote wakefulness in patients with narcolepsy. However, it is considered a second-line treatment due to its sympathomimetic, adverse effects; modafinil is preferred.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Bupropion is incorrect. Bupropion is an antidepressant medication that increases synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine. Though bupropion may have a mildly stimulating effect, bupropion is less likely to promote wakefulness than modafinil and is not typically used in narcolepsy treatment.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Diazepam is incorrect. Diazepam is a benzodiazepine used in the treatment of anxiety, seizures, and alcohol detoxification. Diazepam is not useful in treatment of narcolepsy.</p><p><b>Choice C and Choice E:</b> Imipramine and phenelzine are incorrect. Imipramine is a tricyclic antidepressant. Phenelzine is a nonselective monoamine oxidase inhibitor antidepressant medication. Both are used in the treatment of anxiety and depressive disorders, but not in narcolepsy.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Prolonged isoniazid use without appropriate supplementation can cause vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) deficiency, leading to peripheral neuropathy, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with vitamin B6 deficiency may present with symptoms such as numbness, tingling, and burning sensations in the extremities.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Vitamin B6 deficiency is a significant concern for patients on isoniazid therapy for pulmonary tuberculosis. Isoniazid inhibits the conversion of pyridoxine to its active form, pyridoxal 5'-phosphate, which is essential for amino acid metabolism and neurotransmitter synthesis. This deficiency commonly leads to peripheral neuropathy, characterized by symptoms such as paresthesia, numbness, and pain. Additionally, it can cause sideroblastic anemia as a result of impaired heme synthesis and may increase cardiovascular risk by increasing homocysteine concentrations.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Prophylactic administration of pyridoxine is recommended for all patients receiving isoniazid, especially those at higher risk, such as pregnant patients, individuals with diabetes mellitus, and those with malnutrition.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> If deficiency of pyridoxine occurs, supplementation should be provided to alleviate symptoms and prevent further complications.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with 3 months of worsening paresthesias in the setting of treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis for 1 year. The current regimen includes isoniazid, which is known to lead to vitamin B6 deficiency without proper supplementation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, F, G, H, I, and J.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Niacin is incorrect. Niacin deficiency, known as pellagra, presents with the classic triad of dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Symptoms of pellagra can be exacerbated by the use of isoniazid.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Vitamin A is incorrect. Vitamin A deficiency often presents with night blindness and, in severe cases, can lead to xerophthalmia and keratomalacia.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Vitamin B1 (thiamine) is incorrect. Vitamin B1 deficiency can lead to beriberi, characterized by symptoms such as peripheral neuropathy and heart failure, or Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, particularly in patients with chronic alcohol use disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) is incorrect. Vitamin B2 deficiency presents with symptoms like cheilosis, glossitis, and seborrheic dermatitis.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is incorrect. Vitamin B12 deficiency typically presents with megaloblastic anemia and neurologic symptoms, including paresthesia and ataxia.</p><p><b>Choice G:</b> Vitamin C is incorrect. Vitamin C deficiency, known as scurvy, presents with symptoms like gingival bleeding, petechiae, and impaired wound healing.</p><p><b>Choice H:</b> Vitamin D is incorrect. Vitamin D deficiency can present with rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults, characterized by bone pain and muscle weakness.</p><p><b>Choice I:</b> Vitamin E is incorrect. Vitamin E deficiency can lead to neurologic problems, such as ataxia and peripheral neuropathy, as well as hemolytic anemia.</p><p><b>Choice J:</b> Vitamin K is incorrect. Vitamin K deficiency can present with bleeding tendencies caused by impaired blood coagulation, often recognizable by easy bruising and prolonged bleeding. This can be seen in patients on prolonged antibiotics as a result of destruction of vitamin K-producing organisms within the gut biome.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient presenting with signs of chorea (random, involuntary movements of the face and limbs), along with psychiatric and cognitive symptoms, most likely has Huntington disease (HD). HD is a hereditary, progressive, neurodegenerative disorder associated with atrophy of the caudate nuclei in the basal ganglia. The most appropriate next step in management is to confirm the diagnosis via genetic testing.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> HD is a hereditary, progressive neurodegenerative disorder that commonly arises between the ages of 30 to 50 years, though onset can occur anywhere from childhood to age 80 years. It is associated with atrophy of the caudate nuclei in the basal ganglia, which can be seen on MRI of the brain. HD is caused by the expansion of a cytosine-adenine-guanine (CAG) trinucleotide repeat on chromosome 4p, a process which occurs when a series of three nucleotides in repetition are replicated incorrectly, and too many repeats are included in the replicated product. HD also demonstrates anticipation, a genetic inheritance phenomenon in which future generations are affected at a younger age or with more severe symptoms. In patients with clinical features of HD, genetic testing confirms the diagnosis.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> HD usually presents with an insidious onset of psychiatric (irritability, depression, psychosis) and cognitive symptoms. Motor symptoms (chorea, hypotonia, dystonia, loss of voluntary control of movements) typically appear later in the course of the disease. People with this disease typically die 10 to 40 years after onset.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Once inherited, there is no known strategy for preventing HD.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment is symptomatic and can include tetrabenazine or deutetrabenazine, which target chorea by depleting dopamine stores.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The differential diagnosis for chorea includes benign familial chorea, basal ganglia stroke, and certain neurodegenerative diseases. Tardive dyskinesia from chronic exposure to dopamine antagonists can appear like chorea, but these movements are sometimes more stereotyped, while chorea is more flowing and random.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Carbidopa-levodopa therapy is incorrect. Carbidopa-levodopa therapy assists with Parkinson disease treatment. Parkinson disease presents with bradykinesia and tremor (typically an asymmetric tremor of the upper extremity) rather than widespread involuntary movements. Early psychiatric symptoms are more common in HD than Parkinson disease.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Electromyography is incorrect. Electromyography is useful for the diagnosis of various neuromuscular and inflammatory disorders, such as polymyositis, myasthenia gravis, or amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> MRI of the brain is incorrect. Though MRI of the brain can help exclude structural causes of focal chorea and may show caudate and frontal atrophy in HD, genetic testing is a more reliable confirmatory test.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Slit-lamp ophthalmologic examination is incorrect. Slit-lamp ophthalmologic examination can assist in diagnosis of hepatolenticular degeneration (Wilson disease), which rarely presents with chorea. However, parkinsonism is more common than chorea in Wilson disease.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The neurologic findings in this patient are consistent with common peroneal palsy, which is characterized by weakness in foot dorsiflexion and eversion and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with common peroneal nerve injury typically present with footdrop and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot and lateral leg.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Common peroneal palsy secondary to trauma often presents with a combination of motor and sensory deficits due to injury of the common peroneal nerve, which is most vulnerable at the fibular neck. Motor symptoms include foot drop, characterized by weakness in dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot, leading to a high-stepping gait as the patient attempts to avoid tripping. On physical examination, there is notable weakness in the muscles innervated by the deep peroneal nerve, such as tibialis anterior, and those innervated by the superficial peroneal nerve, including the peroneus longus and brevis, resulting in impaired foot eversion and toe extension. Sensory examination shows diminished sensation over the dorsum of the foot and lateral aspect of the lower leg, corresponding to the sensory distribution of the superficial peroneal nerve, and the first web space, which is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Preventive measures include avoiding prolonged leg crossing, using protective padding in casts or braces, and ensuring proper positioning during surgeries to minimize nerve compression.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment involves physical therapy, use of ankle-foot orthoses to support footdrop, and addressing the underlying cause of compression; surgical intervention may be considered in severe or refractory cases.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with low back, right hip, thigh, and knee pain, with an examination showing normal deep tendon reflexes and a right footdrop. The sensory deficits to the lateral leg and dorsum of the foot, intact ability to invert the ankle, and negative imaging helps to localize this lesion to the level of the common fibular nerve.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> L5 radiculopathy is incorrect. Acute L5 radiculopathy can exhibit weakness in dorsiflexion, toe extension, eversion, and potentially hip abduction. While this patient displays most of these findings except hip abduction weakness, it would be uncommon for an acute L5 radiculopathy to be present without acute radiographic abnormalities, especially in a young male patient.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lumbosacral plexopathy is incorrect. Lumbosacral plexopathy often leads to asymmetrical lower extremity weakness affecting multiple muscle groups, sensory loss in various distributions, and diminished or absent patellar and Achilles reflexes, depending on the specific nerves involved.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Sciatic mononeuropathy is incorrect. Sciatic mononeuropathy presents with weakness in knee flexion, foot dorsiflexion, and plantarflexion, along with sensory loss in the posterior leg and entire foot, sparing the anterior thigh; the Achilles reflex may be diminished or absent.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Tibial mononeuropathy is incorrect. Tibial mononeuropathy is characterized by weakness in plantarflexion and inversion of the foot and sensory deficits on the sole of the foot.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient's presentation is most suggestive of carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning. CO has an affinity for hemoglobin that is hundreds of times greater than that of oxygen, readily displacing oxygen from heme. Symptoms of CO poisoning include manifestations of hypoxia; however, pulse oximetry readings may be falsely normal. Treatment for CO poisoning begins with the administration of 100% oxygen and may require hyperbaric oxygen if symptoms are severe.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> CO poisoning occurs as a consequence of inhaling the gas, often due to its generation in an area of poor ventilation (eg, a garage with door shut and car running, a space heater in a poorly ventilated room, a malfunctioning basement furnace). CO is a byproduct of combustion. Symptoms of CO poisoning include headache, loss of consciousness, dizziness, vomiting, and fatigue; in severe cases, seizures, dysrhythmias, coma, and death may occur. Physical examination may show red, flushed skin.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> When inhaled, CO avidly crosses the pulmonary capillary membrane and tightly binds hemoglobin, inducing a conformational change in the molecule and preventing the binding of oxygen. CO has an affinity for hemoglobin that is hundreds of times greater than that of oxygen, readily displacing oxygen from heme. CO also has an affinity with the mitochondrial electron transport chain; thus, in addition to hypoxemia due to interference with oxygen carrying capacity, cells may not be able to make use of the oxygen they do receive via aerobic metabolism. As a result, anaerobic metabolism dominates, and lactic acidosis caused by hypoxia ensues.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> CO poisoning can be prevented by avoiding inappropriate indoor use of grills, heaters, etc., as well as installing CO detectors. Malfunctioning heating systems and poorly ventilated living spaces place patients at higher risk for CO exposure.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment for CO poisoning begins with the administration of 100% oxygen regardless of pulse oximetry readings. If any neurologic symptoms or signs of end-organ dysfunction are present, as in this case, hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be indicated.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's flushed skin, pink oral mucosa and conjunctivae, altered mental status, and lack of focal neurologic findings are most suggestive of CO poisoning.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Botulinum toxin is incorrect. Botulism is an acute disease of the neuromuscular junction caused by ingestion of toxin from Clostridium botulinum. It presents with progressive, symmetric, descending weakness, not flushed skin and loss of consciousness.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Hydrogen cyanide is incorrect. Cyanide inhibits the electron transport chain, leading to lactic acidosis, compensatory respiratory alkalosis, hypotension, lethargy, and bradycardia. Cyanide poisoning causes mydriasis, while CO poisoning does not. CO poisoning would improve with 100% oxygen, while cyanide poisoning would not. Additionally, this patient's residence is more of a risk factor for CO poisoning than for cyanide poisoning.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Methanol is incorrect. Methanol can be metabolized to formic acid, a toxic metabolite that can cause visual disturbances, retinal edema, optic disc hyperemia, gastrointestinal distress, putaminal hemorrhage, and neurologic depression. Accidental ingestion classically occurs with consumption of incorrectly distilled alcoholic beverages or various cleaning/industrial products. Flushed skin and loss of consciousness are more consistent with CO poisoning.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Organophosphates is incorrect. Organophosphate poisoning is caused by increased concentrations of acetylcholine due to the inhibition of acetylcholinesterase. Symptoms of organophosphate poisoning include diarrhea, vomiting, urination, sweating, salivation, bronchospasm, lethargy, and possible coma. Exposure most often occurs from ingestion of gardening/agricultural products or from chemical warfare.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Inhalant use disorder commonly presents with neuropsychiatric symptoms (eg, headache, ataxia, anosmia, slurred speech, mood swings, hallucinations). Perinasal dermatitis, as seen in this patient, can differentiate inhalant use disorder from other substance use disorders.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Inhalant use disorder, which describes the intentional inhalation of vapors from chemical agents (eg, toluene, nitrous oxide, spray paint, glue), is common in children and adolescents. The effects typically begin within seconds of use and persist approximately 30 minutes, representing a faster onset and resolution compared to other recreational drugs.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Inhalants exert effects on multiple organ systems: dermatologic (eg, perioral or perinasal dermatitis with erythro- or pyoderma), otolaryngologic (eg, nosebleeds, rhinorrhea, halitosis), ocular (eg, conjunctival injection), cardiac (eg, dysrhythmia, tachycardia), gastrointestinal (eg, nausea, anorexia), respiratory (eg, wheezing, coughing, sneezing), and neuropsychiatric (eg, headache, ataxia, anosmia, slurred speech, mood swings, hallucinations, central nervous system depression alternating with agitation). Chronic inhalant intoxication can lead to dementia, encephalopathy, and peripheral neuropathy.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Parents and children should be educated about the risks of inhalant use, and guardians should monitor use of solvent-based products.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment is primarily supportive, though some inhalants have specific antidotes (eg, methylene blue for nitrites).</p><p><b>Differential:</b> Episodes of slurred speech and poor coordination can occur with alcohol or benzodiazepine intoxication. However, psychosis and perinasal dermatitis are more consistent with inhalant use disorder. Other hallucinogens (eg, psilocybin) may lead to psychosis but would not typically present with perinasal dermatitis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Alcohol is incorrect. Alcohol is a central nervous system depressant that can cause slurred speech, emotional lability, behavioral disinhibition, ataxia, and memory lapses. However, psychosis and perinasal dermatitis are more consistent with inhalant use disorder.</p><p><b>Choice B and Choice E:</b> Cocaine and methamphetamine are incorrect. Both cocaine and methamphetamine are central nervous system stimulants that cause euphoria and increase sympathetic tone, leading to tachycardia and hypertension, pupillary dilation, and restlessness (rather than lethargy). Psychosis may occur, but these substances are not typically associated with incoordination. Patients who snort these substances can experience epistaxis and nasal crusting, though perinasal and perioral dermatitis are more consistent with inhalant use disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Cannabis is incorrect. Cannabis causes euphoria, cognitive slowing, conjunctival injection, paranoia, hallucinations, and dilated pupils. Incoordination more commonly occurs in inhalant intoxication, and perinasal dermatitis would be atypical of marijuana intoxication.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The infectious agent of this patient's meningitis is most likely Cryptococcus neoformans. Cryptococcal meningitis is a fungal infection primarily affecting immunocompromised individuals, such as patients with HIV infection, AIDS, or those undergoing cancer treatment.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with cryptococcal meningitis often display subacute progression of symptoms like persistent headache, fever, neck stiffness, and altered mental status.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Cryptococcal meningitis is a serious fungal infection of the central nervous system caused by C neoformans, particularly affecting immunocompromised individuals. These patients may experience subacute onset of symptoms, which can progress over a period of weeks. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis in cryptococcal meningitis typically shows increased opening pressure, lymphocytic pleocytosis, increased protein concentrations, and low-to-normal glucose concentrations. Diagnosis is often confirmed by the presence of cryptococcal antigen in CSF or serum, and an India ink preparation of CSF may show encapsulated yeast cells. Imaging studies, such as MRI, might show meningeal enhancement but can also appear normal.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> In high-risk patients, such as those with weakened immune systems, prophylactic antifungal therapy with medications can help prevent the onset of cryptococcal infections.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> The standard treatment for cryptococcal meningitis involves an initial combination of amphotericin B and flucytosine for induction, followed by fluconazole for consolidation and maintenance to prevent recurrence.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with a recent history of chemotherapy and a 14-day history of headaches, neck soreness, and photophobia. Her examination is grossly benign with no overt evidence of infectious process. Her symptoms are consistent with subacute meningitis, of which C neoformans is the most likely infectious agent.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Aspergillus fumigatus is incorrect. Aspergillus species are more commonly associated with pulmonary infections, especially in immunocompromised patients, leading to conditions such as invasive pulmonary aspergillosis.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) is incorrect. Hib is a bacterial cause of acute meningitis, primarily in unvaccinated children. In adults, especially those who are immunocompromised, Hib is a less common cause due to widespread vaccination, and it would be expected to have rapid onset and cause severe disease.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Mycobacterium tuberculosis is incorrect. Tuberculous meningitis is a possibility in immunocompromised patients and can present with subacute symptoms. However, it often involves a more insidious onset with systemic symptoms, such as fever, weight loss, and night sweats, which are not seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Nocardia asteroides is incorrect. N asteroides can cause central nervous system infections, but these are typically characterized by the formation of brain abscesses rather than meningitis.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has transverse myelitis associated with multiple sclerosis (MS), which often produces partial spinal cord lesions and can present with acute or subacute bilateral sensory abnormalities, weakness, and bowel/bladder dysfunction.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with transverse myelitis associated with MS commonly present with acute or subacute onset of bilateral sensory disturbances, motor weakness, and autonomic dysfunction, such as bladder and bowel issues.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Transverse myelitis is a condition characterized by inflammation of a segment of the spinal cord. This inflammation leads to neurologic symptoms that correspond to the affected spinal level. The presence of oligoclonal bands in the cerebrospinal fluid is highly suggestive of an underlying diagnosis of MS. In patients with MS, the lesions causing transverse myelitis are typically partial, affecting only a portion of the spinal cord's cross-section, which distinguishes it from other causes that might involve complete transverse lesions. MRI of the spine in MS-associated transverse myelitis usually shows demyelinating plaques, providing evidence of the disease process.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Preventive strategies focus on managing MS with disease-modifying therapies to decrease the frequency and severity of relapses that could lead to transverse myelitis.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment usually involves high-dose corticosteroids to decrease inflammation and manage symptoms, along with ongoing disease-modifying therapies to address the underlying MS.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with asymmetric sensory and motor deficits to her bilateral lower extremities, as well as sensory deficits below T9, and bladder dysfunction. On examination, she additionally has upper motor neuron signs evidenced by present Babinski sign and hyperreflexia. These findings are consistent with transverse myelitis, with oligoclonal banding present making MS the most likely cause.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Herniated nucleus pulposus is incorrect. Patients with herniated nucleus pulposus typically present with localized back pain radiating down the leg due to nerve root compression, often accompanied by numbness or weakness in the affected dermatomes. Presence of oligoclonal banding on cerebrospinal fluid analysis would not be expected.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Meningitis is incorrect. Patients with meningitis usually present with a sudden onset of fever, headache, neck stiffness, and altered mental status, sometimes accompanied by photophobia and a characteristic rash in certain types. This patient has none of these signs or symptoms.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Neuromyelitis optica is incorrect. Neuromyelitis optica often presents with severe episodes of optic neuritis, leading to vision loss, and transverse myelitis. This patient does not report any visual concerns, and oligoclonal banding present on cerebrospinal fluid analysis would be unexpected.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Thrombosis of anterior spinal artery is incorrect. Patients with thrombosis of anterior spinal artery commonly present with sudden onset of paraplegia or quadriplegia, loss of pain and temperature sensation, and preserved proprioception and vibration sense due to the characteristic pattern of spinal cord ischemia. Presence of oligoclonal banding on cerebrospinal fluid analysis would be unexpected in these patients.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has botulism, a potentially fatal condition caused by Clostridium botulinum, which leads to descending paralysis and respiratory failure. Prompt administration of botulinum antitoxin is the most appropriate next step, along with supportive care.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Botulism is a severe neuroparalytic condition caused by botulinum toxin as a result of infection with C botulinum, leading to descending flaccid paralysis by inhibiting acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction through the cleavage of soluble N-ethylmaleimide-sensitive fusion attachment protein receptor (SNARE) proteins.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Clinically, botulism is characterized by symmetric, descending flaccid paralysis, initially manifesting with cranial nerve dysfunction, which may include diplopia, ptosis, dysarthria, and dysphagia. As the condition progresses, generalized muscle weakness and respiratory compromise occur because of diaphragmatic and accessory muscle involvement. At the cellular level, botulinum toxin exerts its effects by binding to peripheral cholinergic nerve terminals, where it is internalized and cleaves SNARE proteins, specifically targeting synaptobrevin, synaptosomal-associated protein 25 kDa (SNAP-25), or syntaxin. This cleavage inhibits the fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, preventing the release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Preventing botulism involves proper food handling and preparation techniques, such as thoroughly cooking food, practicing safe canning methods, and avoiding consumption of foods from bulging or damaged cans.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment of botulism involves the administration of antitoxin to neutralize the toxin, along with supportive care, which may include respiratory support and intensive care monitoring.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents less than 1 day after onset of a food borne illness with proximal muscle weakness, ptosis, mydriasis, and extraocular weakness. She subsequently develops descending paralysis, which is characteristic of botulism.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Atropine is incorrect. Atropine is an anticholinergic agent used to treat conditions like bradycardia or organophosphate poisoning by blocking muscarinic receptors, thus decreasing acetylcholine activity. Botulinum toxin inhibits acetylcholine release, so use of atropine may exacerbate parasympathetic signs and symptoms of botulism. Therefore, atropine is not recommended as treatment for botulism.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Ciprofloxacin is incorrect. Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic used to treat bacterial infections, such as urinary tract infections. This patient is experiencing foodborne botulism, and there is no evidence that antibiotics improve outcomes in these cases. In addition, there is a theoretical risk that inappropriate use of antibiotics can lyse bacteria and release additional botulinum toxin. Antibiotics are likely only of value in wound botulism if there are clear signs of a wound infection. If given, antibiotics should ideally be administered only after antitoxin administration and wound debridement and should be broad spectrum due to the potential risk of polymicrobial infections. Ciprofloxacin's spectrum of activity is inadequate for typical wound infections. Finally, antibiotics that have the potential to worsen neuromuscular blockage (eg, aminoglycosides) should be avoided.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Pyridostigmine is incorrect. Pyridostigmine is a cholinesterase inhibitor that increases acetylcholine availability to improve neuromuscular transmission in disorders like myasthenia gravis. By increasing acetylcholine availability, pyridostigmine may provide limited therapeutic effect for the sequelae of botulinum toxin in some patients. However, it does not reverse botulinum toxin's inhibition of acetylcholine release, nor are there robust data to support the routine use of pyridostigmine in any form of botulism. Therefore, it is not first-line treatment for botulism.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Ribavirin is incorrect. Ribavirin is an antiviral medication used to treat viral infections, such as hepatitis C or respiratory syncytial virus. Botulism is caused by a neurotoxin as a result of bacterial infection, so an antiviral like ribavirin has no role in its treatment.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Dermatomyositis is the most likely cause of this patient's fever, myalgias, and rash involving the periorbital area, knees, and knuckles.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Dermatomyositis presents with cutaneous findings, proximal muscle weakness, and myalgias. Common cutaneous findings of dermatomyositis include a heliotrope rash of the periorbital area, Gottron papules overlying the joints of the digits, and shawl sign associated with light pink to lilac-colored patches with telangiectasias involving the sun-exposed areas of the chest and back. Additionally, juvenile dermatomyositis patients often develop calcinosis cutis, associated with deposition of calcium in the skin. Inflammation of the muscle causes damage to the myocytes, leading to release of creatine kinase, which may be used to monitor disease progress. Patients are diagnosed with dermatomyositis based on clinical and serologic findings, including presence of autoantibodies.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Dermatomyositis is an autoimmune condition associated with the development of antibodies and inflammation of the muscles and skin. Patients may show a positive antinuclear antibody, anti-Jo-1, anti-SRP, or anti-Mi-2 antibodies. Dermatomyositis is less likely to be associated with malignancy in children than in adults. All patients diagnosed with dermatomyositis should undergo age-appropriate cancer screening to rule out associated occult malignancy.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There are no preventive treatments for dermatomyositis. Age-appropriate cancer screening in adults may prevent occult malignancy associated with dermatomyositis.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> In children and adults with malignancy-associated dermatomyositis, treatment of malignancy is paramount. Cutaneous findings of dermatomyositis may respond to topical corticosteroids. Systemic symptoms and myositis are managed with immunosuppressants and systemic corticosteroids.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In this patient with fever, myalgia, heliotrope rash, and scaly rash over the knees and knuckles, the most likely diagnosis is dermatomyositis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Acute rheumatic fever is incorrect. Rheumatic fever is a sequela of untreated group A Streptococcus infection in which there is an autoimmune reaction against host proteins that have similar structure to streptococcal M protein. Manifestations of rheumatic fever include carditis, arthritis, subcutaneous nodules, erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Juvenile idiopathic arthritis is incorrect. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis occurs before age 16 years and classically presents with joint pain and inflammation along with extra-articular findings such as uveitis, lymphadenopathy, and rash. While juvenile idiopathic arthritis may present with rash, dermatomyositis is the most likely cause of this patient's presentation.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Septic arthritis is incorrect. Septic arthritis is a common surgical emergency in the pediatric population. Septic arthritis presents with acute joint pain, erythema, swelling, inability to bear weight on the joint, and signs and symptoms of sepsis.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Systemic lupus erythematosus is incorrect. Systemic lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune disorder with a wide range of presentations. Cutaneous manifestations include an erythematous rash over the malar cheeks that spares the nasolabial folds after sun exposure or discoid lesions with hyperpigmented edges and pink, atrophic centers. Painless oral ulcers and hair loss may occur. Fatigue, joint pain, and fever are common presenting signs and symptoms.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The diagnostic study most likely to confirm the cause of this patient's symptoms is MRI of the lumbosacral spine. MRI is the diagnostic imaging modality of choice for detecting potential metastatic spine lesions due to its superior ability to visualize soft tissue and neural structures.</p><p><b>Key Concept:</b> Lung cancer metastasis to the lumbosacral spine typically presents with progressive back pain, potential neurologic deficits, and symptoms indicative of nerve root compression.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> Lung cancer frequently metastasizes to the lumbosacral spine, presenting a serious clinical challenge due to its potential for causing significant neurologic complications and pain. Patients often report a gradual onset of deep, aching back pain that worsens with movement and may radiate along the dermatomes, indicating nerve root involvement. Neurologic symptoms such as weakness, numbness, or even bowel and bladder dysfunction can occur if the spinal cord or cauda equina is compressed. The primary risk factor for developing lung cancer, and consequently for metastasis to the spine, is smoking, with non-small cell lung cancer being particularly prone to bone metastases. Physical examination may disclose focal tenderness over the affected vertebrae, and signs of radiculopathy or myelopathy may be present, depending on the extent of the metastatic spread. Imaging is crucial for diagnosis; MRI provides detailed visualization of metastatic lesions and any spinal cord compression, while CT scans are useful for assessing bone integrity and detecting any potential fractures or structural compromise.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with a long history of cigarette smoking as well as new back pain, bladder dysfunction, nonproductive cough, and weight loss. These findings are consistent with lung cancer with potential metastases to the spine.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> CT scan of the thoracic spine is incorrect. While CT scans are excellent for evaluating bone integrity and detecting fractures, they are limited in their ability to distinguish soft tissue contrast and neural involvement. Additionally, imaging of the thoracic spine is unlikely to visualize a mass, as this patient's symptom origin is more inferior.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Electromyography and nerve conduction studies is incorrect. Electromyography and nerve conduction studies are diagnostic tools for assessing electrical activity and function of muscles and peripheral nerves, which do not provide direct information about the presence or extent of metastatic lesions in the spine.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lumbar puncture for examination of cerebrospinal fluid is incorrect. A lumbar puncture should be avoided in this patient as this risks potentially hitting a mass that is present and further seeding the malignant cells. Additionally, cerebrospinal fluid analysis offers little to no benefit in the workup of this patient.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Serum alkaline phosphatase activity is incorrect. While increased serum alkaline phosphatase activity can suggest bone turnover, this is a nonspecific marker that would not confirm the diagnosis.</p>", "1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Central sleep apnea should be expected in this patient with paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, in association with inadequate sleep quality and daily headaches, following an ischemic pontine stroke. The diagnosis can be confirmed with polysomnography.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Central sleep apnea often presents in older adults with poor sleep quality, daytime somnolence, morning headaches, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, or witnessed nocturnal apnea. Central sleep apnea occurs more often in patients with a history of stroke, congestive heart failure, and long-term use of opioid pain medications.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Central sleep apnea occurs due to a decrease in the ventilatory drive during sleep. Central sleep apnea is less common than obstructive sleep apnea, which occurs due to repetitive obstruction of the pharyngeal airway during sleep as a result of a combination of structural, vascular, and neuromuscular factors. With both types of sleep apnea, patients become hypoxemic several times nightly and experience fragmented sleep, leading to daytime sleepiness. The diagnosis of sleep apnea can be confirmed with polysomnography.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> There are no specific measures to prevent the development of central sleep apnea.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> First-line treatment consists of continuous positive airway pressure, occasionally with the addition of supplemental oxygen.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient's symptoms suggest a sleep disorder rather than anxiety or post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), which could be treated with cognitive behavioral therapy, prazosin, or clonazepam. While mefloquine can be associated with nightmares, sleep apnea should first be ruled out in this patient with risk factors for central sleep apnea. Neurocognitive testing is not warranted, as it would be unlikely to explain the cause of this patient's interrupted sleep.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cognitive behavioral therapy is incorrect. Cognitive behavioral therapy is an excellent treatment modality for anxiety, depression, obsessive compulsive disorder, and PTSD, in addition to a variety of other mental health issues. While this patient is experiencing insomnia or interrupted sleep due to recurrent nightmares, a medical cause should first be sought and ruled out prior to initiating cognitive behavioral therapy.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Discontinuing mefloquine is incorrect. Mefloquine is a prophylactic agent used to prevent malaria infection in those patients who travel to endemic areas. Mefloquine has been associated with anxiety and vivid dream states. However, given this patient's recurrent travel to areas in which malaria is endemic, other causes of his symptoms should be ruled out before discontinuing malaria prophylaxis.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Formal neurocognitive testing is incorrect. Formal neurocognitive testing is most used to identify dementia, brain injury, or psychological disorders, such as attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Though this patient expresses difficulties separating dream from reality, this is more likely due to sleep latency immediately after awakening rather than a primary neurocognitive disorder.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Initiating clonazepam is incorrect. Clonazepam is a benzodiazepine used in the treatment of anxiety, panic disorder, and seizures. Clonazepam is often prescribed to treat insomnia. Prescription of a benzodiazepine in this patient with central sleep apnea could be disastrous, as it would likely worsen central apneic episodes due to its sedative effects.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Initiating prazosin is incorrect. Prazosin is an α1-adrenergic receptor antagonist that is used in the treatment of hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia. Prazosin has also shown efficacy in patients with nightmares associated with PTSD. This patient's nightmares are more likely related to his periods of apnea than an underlying diagnosis of PTSD.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient with AIDS presents with progressive bilateral vision loss and funduscopic examination notable for exudates with hemorrhage. This presentation is most suggestive of cytomegalovirus retinitis.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Cytomegalovirus retinitis is an opportunistic infection that affects immunocompromised patients, especially those with HIV and CD4+ T-lymphocyte counts less than 50/mm³. Cytomegalovirus invades the retina and causes bilateral, progressive destruction that presents as vision loss. On fundoscopy, retinal edema, exudates, and hemorrhages are characteristic.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> The diagnosis of cytomegalovirus retinitis is clinical. A history of AIDS-defining illness (in this case, central nervous system toxoplasmosis), or low CD4+ T-lymphocyte count, along with a funduscopic examination notable for exudates and hemorrhage is consistent with cytomegalovirus retinitis. Cytomegalovirus retinitis is not generally seen in immunocompetent patients.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> The use of highly active antiretroviral therapy has significantly decreased the rate of cytomegalovirus retinitis. Patients with low CD4+ T-lymphocyte counts should receive regular ophthalmic screening.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Treatment includes antiviral medications such as ganciclovir.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> The differential diagnosis includes other causes of infectious retinitis, such as herpes retinitis, along with other retinal diseases such as diabetic retinopathy. This patient's funduscopic findings narrow the affected structure to the retina.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, E, F, and H.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Basilar artery is incorrect. Basilar artery pathology classically presents with a wide range of symptoms including motor deficits, dysarthria, dizziness, and diplopia. Basilar artery disease does not have funduscopic examination findings of exudates and bilateral hemorrhage. Furthermore, the vision changes associated with basilar artery pathology such as stroke would be rapid in onset.</p><p><b>Choice B and Choice H:</b> Left optic nerve and right optic nerve are incorrect. Left optic nerve lesions would present with monocular vision loss in the left eye, and right optic nerve lesions would present with monocular vision loss in the right eye. This patient has bilateral vision loss.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Lens is incorrect. Lens pathology, for example cataracts, presents with a gradual decline in vision and can be bilateral; however, the funduscopic examination would not show exudates or hemorrhage, as seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Occipital lobe is incorrect. Lesions within the occipital lobe, such as a stroke, would present with visual field defects. Most commonly, this would present with homonymous hemianopia. The funduscopic examination would not show exudates or hemorrhage.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Ophthalmic artery is incorrect. Occlusion of the ophthalmic artery presents with a sudden profound loss of vision in the affected eye, which is typically unilateral and acute. This patient's presentation is more consistent with retinitis.</p><p><b>Choice F:</b> Optic chiasm is incorrect. Lesions involving the optic chiasm present with bitemporal hemianopia, or loss of vision in the outer visual fields of both eyes. This patient does not have visual field deficits.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: This patient most likely has epidural spinal cord metastasis, also known as metastatic epidural spinal cord compression (MESSC). Spinal cord metastases are a common cause of spinal cord compression in patients with cancer, often resulting in upper or lower motor neuron symptoms, depending on the level of involvement.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Patients with malignancy that is metastatic to the spinal cord typically present with back pain along with the possibility to develop motor weakness, sensory changes, and signs of upper motor neuron dysfunction, such as hyperreflexia and a present Babinski sign.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Lung cancer is a common primary malignancy that frequently metastasizes to the spinal cord, potentially leading to MESSC. When lung cancer metastasizes to the epidural space, patients may experience severe back pain, which is often described as persistent and progressive, typically worse at night, and may be exacerbated by movement. Neurologic symptoms, such as motor weakness, sensory deficits, or changes in bowel and bladder function, can also occur, reflecting the level of spinal cord involvement. On physical examination, focal tenderness over the affected vertebrae may be present, and a neurologic examination may show upper or lower motor neuron signs depending on the extent and location of the compression. Imaging studies, particularly MRI of the spine, are crucial in identifying the extent of metastatic involvement and assessing any resultant spinal cord compression.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> While primary prevention of MESSC is not possible, early detection and treatment of primary lung cancer can help decrease the risk for metastatic spread.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Management of MESSC includes corticosteroids to decrease edema, radiation therapy, and surgical decompression, with systemic therapy tailored to the primary cancer type.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> This patient presents with bilateral leg weakness, increasing thoracic spine pain, weight loss, nonproductive cough for 6 months, and an examination showing weakness and upper motor neuron signs. X-rays of the spine show destructive lesions at T2 and T10, all of which are consistent with malignancy with metastatic spread to the spinal cord.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice A:</b> Cauda equina syndrome is incorrect. Cauda equina syndrome typically presents with severe lower back pain, saddle anesthesia, bowel and bladder dysfunction, and lower motor neuron signs in the bilateral lower extremities. This patient shows upper motor neuron signs.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Epidural abscess is incorrect. An epidural abscess often presents with severe localized back pain, fever, and neurologic deficits, such as radicular pain or weakness, which can progress to paralysis if not promptly treated. This patient does not have a fever, and her signs and symptoms are more consistent with MESSC.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Lumbosacral plexopathy is incorrect. Lumbosacral plexopathy presents with asymmetric lower extremity weakness, sensory disturbances, and pain, often affecting the buttocks, thighs, and legs, depending on the specific nerves involved. Lower motor neuron signs would be expected, rather than the upper motor neuron signs seen in this patient.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Vertebral osteomyelitis is incorrect. Vertebral osteomyelitis typically presents with insidious onset of back pain, localized tenderness over the affected vertebrae, fever, and occasionally neurologic deficits if there is spinal cord or nerve root involvement. This patient does not have a fever, and her signs and symptoms are more consistent with MESSC.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: When a medication has a high likelihood of benefiting a patient but carries a risk for a rare, serious adverse effect, physicians should consider initiating the medication and monitoring for the rare adverse effect. Levetiracetam is commonly associated with mild psychiatric symptoms, and occasionally, serious psychiatric symptoms. In this case, it is most appropriate for the physician to advise the patient that the benefits of levetiracetam outweigh the risks and monitor for signs of depression.</p><p><b>Evaluation:</b> Levetiracetam-induced depression presents with symptoms typical of major depressive disorder, such as low mood, anhedonia, difficulty concentrating, feelings of guilt, appetite changes, and neurovegetative symptoms. Severe levetiracetam-induced depression is uncommon; however, mild to moderate depressive symptoms are more common and should be closely monitored.</p><p><b>Definition:</b> Levetiracetam is a broad-spectrum antiepileptic medication with a wide therapeutic window, making it easy to use and well tolerated. Levetiracetam is thought to act by binding to synaptic vesicle glycoprotein 2A (SV2A), resulting in neuronal inhibition, increased γ-aminobutyric acid signaling, and decreased glutamatergic activation. Mild psychiatric symptoms (eg, depression) are a common adverse effect. However, severe psychiatric symptoms (eg, psychosis, anger outbursts, suicidality) only occur occasionally. Physicians should balance the risks and benefits of any medication. Given the risk for suicidality is relatively rare and levetiracetam is an effective antiepileptic medication, the benefits appear to outweigh the risks, and patients can be monitored for suicidality.</p><p><b>Prevention:</b> Limiting the dose of levetiracetam could decrease the risk for psychiatric adverse effects, but some studies have shown that psychiatric adverse effects are not dose dependent. There is no specific known preventive measure, apart from not initiating levetiracetam.</p><p><b>Treatment:</b> Decreasing the dose or discontinuing the medication can help address levetiracetam-induced psychiatric effects. However, some patients may need to continue levetiracetam or may occasionally experience persistent serious psychiatric effects after levetiracetam is discontinued, which may necessitate use of antidepressant or antipsychotic medication, depending on the symptoms present.</p><p><b>Differential:</b> In patients with a history of serious mental illness or behavioral disturbances, or who strongly prefer to avoid psychiatric adverse effects, other antiepileptic medications may be considered. For example, valproic acid may be considered for patients who experience agitation or aggression, or who have a history of bipolar disorder, as valproic acid can address both epilepsy and these psychiatric symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E.</p><p><b>Choice B:</b> Advise the patient that escitalopram is a preventative therapy for major depressive disorder is incorrect. Escitalopram has not been shown to have prophylactic use in this patient population.</p><p><b>Choice C:</b> Discontinue levetiracetam because the risk of recurrent seizure is low is incorrect. This patient shows epileptogenicity on EEG, indicating a high risk for seizure recurrence that necessitates treatment with antiepileptic medication.</p><p><b>Choice D:</b> Initiate low-dose lorazepam for anxiety is not the most appropriate next step in management. Though this patient expresses an understandable concern about levetiracetam, he does not demonstrate clinically significant anxiety, and offering an anxiety medication is likely to invalidate his concern.</p><p><b>Choice E:</b> Initiate vigabatrin and discontinue levetiracetam is incorrect. Vigabatrin is typically used in refractory epilepsy cases only because it is associated with significant adverse effects, such as visual field defects.</p>"};
